/* Source-page English is first-pass, only partially literal and not all-register complete. */
window.GNAMMA_SANANDAJI_I=(window.GNAMMA_SANANDAJI_I||[]).concat([{"page":2,"body":"— Opening invocation and praise\n*Original PDF physical page 15 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Compassionate. Praise be to the One who guided us to the path of Islam: praise commensurate with His favours. Thanks be to the One who granted us success in refining theological discourse and bringing its objectives within reach: thanks adequate to His increase of bounty. May blessings be continually invoked, for as long as the traces of the Islamic benefits endure upon the pages of the days, upon our master and Prophet Muhammad, whom God sent to all people and chose to subdue error and raise the banner of guidance; and upon his pure family. [The invocation continues at the beginning of the next leaf.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nالحمد من هدانا إلى طريق الإسلام · حمدا يوافي نعمه · وشكر الذي وفقنا على تهذيب الكلام وتقريب المرام · على سيدنا ونبينا محمد\n\n**Reading caution:** Highly ornate rhymed and partly metrical prose; uncertain ornamental epithets are not presented as a certified verbatim transcription. Middle and lower printed commentary are separate voices.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":3,"body":"— Sanandajī names himself and explains the need for a commentary\n*Original PDF physical page 16 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\n[Blessings also upon] his honoured and elect Companions. To proceed: the servant who stands in need of the kindness of his Lord, who is rich beyond all need, 'Abd al-Qadir, son of Shaykh Muhammad al-Sa'id al-Takhti, says: The section devoted to kalam in the Tahdhib, composed by the exemplar of scholars, that second great master Sa'd al-Din al-Taftazani—may his spirit be sanctified—combines firmness of construction and powerful concision with unusual intellectual insights that the passage of time has not effaced, and precious subtleties whose true beauties have not yet all been disclosed. Although I was immersed in the encumbrances of my time and its disturbances, someone whose wishes I could not properly oppose, and with whom agreement was incumbent on me, urged me to explain it and to disclose what it contains. I therefore undertook to draw out its intended meanings and the secrets of its expressions, in response to that request. [The syntactic conclusion of this appeal continues on I:4.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nفيقول العبد الفقير إلى ألطاف ربه الغني · عبد القادر ابن الشيخ محمد السعيد التختي · لما كان قسم التهذيب من الكلام · مشتملا على غرائب أفكار\n\n**Reading caution:** The original has rhymed phrases, compressed literary descriptions and a continuation into I:4; English retains the argumentative movement, not every rhyme.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":4,"body":"— Purpose and modesty of the commentary; praise of his teacher\n*Original PDF physical page 17 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\n[I undertook] to elucidate its expressions and bring its meanings close enough that their difficulties could be made intelligible, like pearls released from their shells. The result, by God's praise, was a commentary that would suffice for its intended aim: a clarification of rare points, an opening of the obscurities and a removal of difficulties, as far as the writer was able. I hope it will be found free of damaging defects and deficiencies, but I submit it to the reader's examination rather than insist on my own judgment. If you remain doubtful about what has been claimed, look again and return your gaze: do you see any flaw? All of this I ascribe to the overflowing instruction of my father and teacher, whose counsel and benefit lie behind this work. May God continue to grant him happiness and increase his standing. [The accompanying verse of supplication and the naming of the work continue onto I:5.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nتوضيح عباراته · فجاء بحمد الله ذلك شرحا · خالياً عن شوائب الخلل والنقصان · فانظر ثم ارجع البصر هل ترى من فطور\n\n**Reading caution:** The source is intensely ornate. The sentence beginning “فانظر ثم ارجع البصر” echoes Qur’anic wording; it is presented as the author’s quotation/allusion, not as independent proof of textual perfection.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":5,"body":"— Title of the commentary and closing prayer\n*Original PDF physical page 18 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nMay [my teacher] ever remain, as his name suggests, happy, through the rank of the master of humankind, Ahmad. Having found this commentary deserving a name that expresses its aim, I called it Taqrib al-Maram fi Sharh Tahdhib al-Kalam—Bringing the Intended Aim Near in the Commentary on the Refinement of Kalam. I ask God to make it beneficial to those who seek it, by the sanctity of our master and exemplar Muhammad, the Hashimite, the Qurashi—may God bless him and his family at every morning and evening.\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nسميته تقريب المرام في شرح تهذيب الكلام · أسأل الله أن ينفعه كل ذي · بحرمة سيدنا ومقتدانا · محمد الهاشمي القرشي\n\n**Reading caution:** The middle and extensive lower texts on this leaf belong to a different register; Wasīm’s I:2–5 identified preface is preserved separately in the earlier edition.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":6,"body":"— The theological section of the Tahdhīb and why the preliminary material belongs here\n*Original PDF physical page 19 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\n“This”—that is, the material which follows—is the science of the section of kalam in the book called the Tahdhib. The expression “section” is used with some latitude, because what is mentioned here may belong either to that which has to precede the investigation of kalam—the first chapter of the introduction—or to the science itself. Once the preliminary discussions have been distinguished from the inquiries particular to the science, the material can be distributed among its proper chapters. The continuation specifies the several subjects that fall under kalam and its preliminary topics. [The list of chapters passes into I:7.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nهذا أي ما سيأتي علم · قسم الكلام من الكتاب المسمى بالتهذيب · وهو الباب الأول في المقدمة\n\n**Reading caution:** Only a short upper paragraph appears on this leaf; the middle and especially lower discussion of the title and nomenclature is substantial and is not included here.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":7,"body":"— Chapters on existence, accidents, substances, prophecy and the divine\n*Original PDF physical page 20 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\n[Among the inquiries are] existence, the subject of the second chapter on general matters; or something particular to the possible existent that does not subsist by itself but in another, which is the third chapter on accidents; or the possible existent which subsists in itself, which belongs to the fourth chapter on substances. As for the Necessary Existent, exalted is He, the inquiry may consider Him with reference to His sending messengers and commissioning prophets; this is the sixth chapter, on the matters known from revelation. Or it may consider Him without that specific respect, and this is the fifth chapter, on divine matters. The preceding chapter on preliminary principles is thus not simply one more species of existent: it prepares for the discussions that follow.\n\nA printed heading then introduces “The first chapter of the introduction.”\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nالباب الثاني في الأمور العامة · الباب الثالث في الأعراض · الباب الرابع في الجواهر · الباب السادس في السمعيات · الباب الخامس في الإلهيات\n\n**Reading caution:** This upper page is mainly an enumeration of chapters. The lower long note debates why logic and preliminary knowledge count as an introduction.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":8,"body":"— Why kalām begins with knowledge, investigation and proof\n*Original PDF physical page 21 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nThe inquiries of kalam generally begin with the discussion of knowledge, rational investigation and demonstration. For the acquisition of doctrinal convictions through investigation and inference, and the reply to those who deny the attainment of knowledge altogether, or its derivation from investigation either absolutely or specifically in divine matters, depend upon these inquiries. They are therefore placed first, in accordance with what is customary as a prolegomenon to any science.\n\n“Kalām is the science of doctrinal convictions”—that is, of judgments whose intended end is the heart's assent to them; this is in contrast with the practical rulings whose intended end includes performance. The “doctrinal convictions” are thus not defined merely by the external subject matter of every proposition about religion, but by the kind of cognitive adherence the science seeks. [The definition continues on I:9.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nمباحث العلم والنظر والدليل · لأن تحصيل العقائد بطريق النظر والاستدلال · الرد على منكري حصول العلم مطلقا · علم الكلام هو العلم بالعقائد\n\n**Reading caution:** The phrase “علم الكلام” and definition are embedded propositions; the surrounding explanation is not all a direct quotation from Taftāzānī. The lower gloss distinguishes knowledge and belief at length.\n\n### Separate lower commentary — author UNASSIGNED\n\nA question may be raised: what is meant by saying that these preliminaries are peculiar to the science of kalam? The intended peculiarity is comparative rather than exclusive, as the author states in the Sharh al-Maqasid. The other Islamic sciences may have reasons to employ some of these discussions; the issue is their special bearing upon the purpose of kalam. One may also say that when the same discussions occur in logic, they are considered insofar as they concern conception and division, or as preparation for explaining logic's own subject. Here, however, their function is to decide whether knowledge is possible, whether it is acquired by consideration, and whether consideration is productive of knowledge without qualification. Under that aspect the inquiry is required for the *commencement* of kalam; the discussions in logic alone do not furnish what is needed for these theological objections.\n\nOn the expression “judgments whose intended end is merely their belief by the heart”: the word “judgment” here means a complete assertoric relation, not a positive legal ruling in the technical usage of jurists. It comprises such propositions as obligation, possibility and other predicates, insofar as they are considered for assent. The intended end is the assent itself, whereas in practical disciplines the purpose is also the manner of acting. This does not entail that a theological proposition can have no effect on action, nor that a practical proposition does not require belief. The distinction is what is primarily sought in the respective disciplines. [The note continues at the end of I:8 into the following page; its first and final compressed expressions need further collation.]\n\n**Textual caution:** This is a connected selective translation of substantial printed lower argument, NOT an all-clauses diplomatic rendering and not a positive historical identification of its author.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":9,"body":"— Doctrinal, religious and certain: qualifications in the definition of kalām\n*Original PDF physical page 22 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nThe intended “convictions” are those connected with religion, as distinct from the rulings of jurisprudence, whose end is practice. They are called *religious* because they are attributed to the religion brought by our Prophet Muhammad—God bless him and grant him peace—whether the doctrine actually belongs to that religion, as in the discourse of those taken to be right, or is only attributed to it, as in the discourse of the heterodox sects discussed by the author. It is attribution to the religion, rather than the truth of every claim made in its name, that supplies the immediate delimitation here.\n\nIf “knowledge” were intended in the entirely unrestricted sense of any assent, whether it corresponds to reality or not, it would also include error. The additional qualification “from certain proofs” would then have to specify the kind of epistemic grounding intended. The text accordingly examines how the reference to *certain evidence* should be understood and whether it refers to what genuinely produces certainty or to what the disputant regards as certain. [The problem is developed on I:10.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nالعقائد الدينية · سواء كانت من الدين في الواقع ككلام أهل الحق · أو لا ككلام الفرق المبتدعة · عن الأدلة اليقينية\n\n**Reading caution:** Literal terms “الفرَق المبتدعة” describe the historical author’s classifications, not editorial endorsement. The lower commentary questions whether belief and demonstrative knowledge have been illicitly conflated.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":10,"body":"— Certainty, evidence, and the scope of the discipline\n*Original PDF physical page 23 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nThe intended sense must be knowledge possessed with certainty. For a proof properly called certain is one that yields certainty, rather than a proof merely supposed to do so by the individual offering it. But it may be answered that the description “certain” is being applied here to the evidence as regarded by the investigator; it does not by itself resolve the status of every opponent's argument.\n\nA further difficulty arises from the scope of the definition. If one speaks simply of knowing doctrinal propositions, the knowledge of God and the Messenger about those propositions, and even the adherence of one who merely follows another, may seem to fall under the expression. Yet the science of kalam is concerned with acquired knowledge obtained through demonstrative consideration, not every occurrence of correct assent or an infallible source's knowing the truth. The role of knowing the arguments is therefore essential to the intended definition. [The concluding qualification passes into I:11.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nالدليل اليقيني · في الاعتقادات · علم الله وعلم الرسول بالاعتقادات · وكذا اعتقاد المقلد\n\n**Reading caution:** Portions of the upper text are compressed in the scan. The lengthy lower text debates the epistemic status of the imitator (muqallid) and degrees of certainty. Not translated here.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":11,"body":"— The subject of the science: anything considered in relation to doctrine\n*Original PDF physical page 24 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nThis applies also to the knowledge of the Companions, although their discourse was not called *kalam* in that earlier period, just as the names of other disciplines were not always in use at the time of their first practice. A discipline is not made to depend upon the historical introduction of its later technical name.\n\n“Its subject is the known, insofar as it bears upon that [end].” That is, the things considered within the science are not confined in advance to a single class of existents. They are taken up under the respect in which knowledge of them bears upon establishing religious doctrine. [The explanation of the subject continues on I:12.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nعلم الصحابة · وموضوعه المعلوم من حيث يتعلق بذلك\n\n**Reading caution:** The main upper text on I:11 is very short. Most of the leaf is a separate extended lower objection concerning subject matter, acquired knowledge and justification.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":12,"body":"— The scope of the known: being, nonbeing and intermediate claims\n*Original PDF physical page 25 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nThe respect in question is the connection with religious convictions. For some inquiries concern states or predicates that rule out beginninglessness or unqualified unity; others concern body and accident, origination and composition, or substantiality, individuation and other categories. Still others concern impossibilities and things that do not exist, such as the impossibility of a second deity. All these matters may serve the establishment of religious doctrine, even though not every one of them is itself an article to be believed as an independent dogma.\n\nIf the subject is described as “the known” (*al-maʿlūm*), this is intended to include what exists and what does not exist, and even what would be regarded as a thing under discussion despite its impossibility. The subject of kalam is therefore broader than a genus of corporeal things, or even than actually existent things. The inquiry remains governed by the regard in which these matters function in the science. [The text continues on I:13.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nأي بالعقائد الدينية · أحوال المانع من القدم والوحدة · أحوال الجسم والعرض والحدوث والتركيب · أحوال المحال والمعدومات · المعلوم المتناول للموجود والمعدوم\n\n**Reading caution:** The upper list is compressed and the reference to impossible/nonexistent states needs further collation. Avoid automatically translating “maʿlūm” as “existent.”","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":13,"body":"— What counts as a theological question: ends and explanatory bases\n*Original PDF physical page 26 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nUnder the respect just stated, the subject takes in the predicates of the questions treated by the science. Thus the intended religious doctrines themselves are included, together with the means by which they are established and the principles upon which their proofs depend. It is not required that every individual question should be one of the doctrines in its final form: the matters leading to their establishment also enter the science under the relevant consideration.\n\nThe end of all this is success in the felicity of the two abodes. The commentator adds that different disciplines may refer to that ultimate end while differing in their proximate subjects and methods. Kalam is distinguished by the doctrinal aim that governs the way its objects are discussed, not by treating every useful religious proposition as a question of kalam. [The account of ends begins to be distinguished further on I:14.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nمن الحيثية المذكورة يتناول محمولات مسائله · العقائد الدينية · الوسائل الموصلة إلى تلك العقائد · غاية الكل الفوز بسعادة الدارين\n\n**Reading caution:** Mixed main text with Taftāzānī lemmas. The marginal analysis of the existence/nonexistence subject is not translated completely.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":14,"body":"— The end of kalām and whether knowledge must be acquired\n*Original PDF physical page 27 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nThe benefit just mentioned is an ultimate end, in relation to which a closer end is sought through the discipline. Knowledge is not merely the presence of a formula in memory: it is the attainment of the appropriate relation to what is known. A question arises whether every instance of knowledge must be acquired by investigation.\n\nThe Imām al-Rāzī favoured the view that some knowledge is necessary rather than acquired, for two reasons. First, a person's awareness that he exists is not normally the result of constructing a demonstration. Second, if every act of knowledge had to be acquired from an earlier act of knowledge, there would be no starting point for learning: either the acquisition would proceed in a circle or it would regress indefinitely. The author distinguishes these contentions from an unqualified claim that *all* theoretical knowledge is immediate. [The remainder of the discussion of necessary and acquired knowledge passes to I:15.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nفيكون غاية كغاية الغاية · والعلم اعتقاد لموضوعه · واختار الإمام الرازي لوجهين · أن علم كل أحد بأنّه موجود ضروري\n\n**Reading caution:** The lower writer offers detailed alternatives on the meaning of “necessary.” The upper is a connected source reading, not exhaustive all-register translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":15,"body":"— Conception, assent and the distinction between acquired and necessary knowledge\n*Original PDF physical page 28 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nThe objection assumes no distinction between having knowledge and conceiving what knowledge is. But the necessary and immediate item may be the actual occurrence of a particular cognition—such as the cognition of one's own existence—whereas one's *conception* of that cognition is something different. The claim that the conception of knowledge requires an earlier conception of knowledge does not follow merely from the occurrence of the cognitive act itself.\n\nFor this reason, the argument from immediate self-awareness must not be made to prove more than it proves. The reality of a cognitive state and one's reflective apprehension of that state are different considerations. Likewise, the regress objection needs to distinguish the knowledge being acquired from the conditions that permit one to acquire it, lest it shift covertly from a claim about knowing a thing to a claim about conceiving the nature of knowing. [The text carries the distinctions into I:16.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nعلى عدم التفرقة بين تصور العلم وحصوله · والضروري حصول علم جزئي بوجوده · وهو غير تصوره · توقف تصوره على تصوره وغير تصور علمه\n\n**Reading caution:** Beginning has inherited context from I:14. The source refers to opponents’ objections and answers; the bracketed articulation in English is explicitly explanatory.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":16,"body":"— What “knowledge” designates: apprehension, judgment and certainty\n*Original PDF physical page 29 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nA second route to determining the meaning of knowledge is by division. Belief may be firm or not firm; a firm judgment may correspond to reality or fail to correspond to it; and a judgment corresponding to reality may be secure or unstable. By these divisions one seeks to separate knowledge from mere conviction, from error and from a judgment held only on unstable grounds.\n\nThe terminology is not uniform. In some usages “knowledge” (*ʿilm*) means the apprehension of a simple object and “recognition” (*maʿrifa*) that of something composite; others reverse or qualify that distinction. Some speak of knowledge as the occurrence of the form of the thing in the intellect; others as firm, reality-corresponding, stable assent. The science is thus confronted with more than one definition and needs to distinguish the intended mode of cognition from the differences introduced by technical usage. [The subsequent proposed definition is examined on I:17.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nوالثاني طريق معرفته عنده القسمة · فإن الاعتقاد إما جازم أو غير جازم · وإما مطابق للواقع أو غير مطابق · فإن العلم إدراك البسيط والمعرفة إدراك المركب · وحصول صورة الشيء في العقل والاعتقاد الجازم المطابق الثابت\n\n**Reading caution:** This leaf includes a long lower controversy over necessity and the division of knowledge. The precise sequence of all propositional cases is still a working reading.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":17,"body":"— Knowledge as complete disclosure and the act of rational consideration\n*Original PDF physical page 30 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nAnother definition makes knowledge an attribute through which an object is disclosed or becomes manifest to the knower, with complete disclosure. The expression “complete” is meant to exclude the uncertainty that belongs to opinion: on this usage knowledge is opposed to conjecture. An apprehension of a simple or particular thing may be called “recognition,” but this is a matter of terminological convention, not a discovery that the reality of knowledge changes according to the names given to it.\n\nThe purpose of mentioning these conventions is not to pretend that the essence of knowledge has thereby been definitively captured. They explain how different authors use the words while leaving the question of a proper real definition open.\n\n“The reality of consideration [*naẓar*] is a movement of the soul”: a transition of the intellect from what it already knows toward something not yet known, by arranging cognitions in such a way as to reach the sought result. [The following page explains the transitions in this process.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nأو صفة يتجلى بها · انكشافا تاما · عند القائلين بهذا التعريف مقابل للظن · ويسمى إدراك البسيط أو الجزئي معرفة · تنبيه على اختلاف الاصطلاحات · وحقيقة النظر حركة النفس\n\n**Reading caution:** The alternative definition and whether it covers simple/compound objects require careful technical comparison. The tiny separate middle gloss is not merged into this upper translation.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":18,"body":"— The stages of consideration and the place of the unknown conclusion\n*Original PDF physical page 31 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\nConsideration takes place among intelligible objects. The intellectual movement has stages: first, attending to what is sought and what is lacking; then turning toward the known matters from which the sought result might be obtained; then arranging those cognitions in the required order; and finally obtaining the previously unknown conclusion. The movement is called a movement of the soul, not because the intellect moves in physical space, but because it proceeds from a present cognition to another act of cognizing.\n\nThe familiar descriptions of a movement *towards* the principles and a *return* from them must not be taken to imply two different physical journeys. They describe the dependence of the result on premises, and the mind's use of those premises in eliciting a conclusion. Since the question is how to acquire what was not already known, a consideration that simply repeats the result from the outset would not perform its purported task. [The lower objections to this arrangement, and a further upper qualification, continue on I:19.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nفي المعقولات · وعودا · الحصول · المجهول · وكونه · لا يقصد أصلا\n\n**Reading caution:** The main upper on this leaf begins mid-sentence and describes the ordering of mental transitions; several tiny connectors remain uncertain; this is not a certified literal reconstruction.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":19,"body":"— Acquired knowledge requires productive consideration; theological exceptions\n*Original PDF physical page 32 · connected_upper_first_pass · NOT independently certified.*\n\n[The prior example concerns] the judgment that one is half of two. Although an act of investigation may be invoked in explaining how an evident proposition is considered, it does not follow that any sequence of propositions whatsoever yields a previously unknown result. Where investigation is said to generate knowledge, the conditions under which its premises genuinely supply a new cognition must be satisfied.\n\n“In divine matters” the difficulty is sharper, because some disputants deny that consideration can produce knowledge there, whether from the nature of the objects or from the limits of inference. The author distinguishes what is known immediately from what requires a proof, and asks whether the necessity attached to knowledge in some cases supports a sweeping denial of proof in theology. The question is not settled by the mere occurrence of disagreement: disagreement may arise even around propositions asserted to be necessary. [The final clause crosses into I:20, and the lower writer's extensive objection is not fully translated.]\n\n**Source anchors (selected original Arabic, not a full diplomatic transcription)**\n\nوكون ذلك الواحد نصف الاثنين · وفي الإلهيات · ولا يقتضي العلم إلا إذا تحققت الحقائق · وبدون العلم · ضروري · الاحتياج إلى بيان\n\n**Reading caution:** The first phrase is securely readable but the compressed clause linking the theological objection to the next page remains provisional. The upper text continues on I:20.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":20,"body":"— The possibility of disagreement over necessities\n**Physical PDF 33.**\n\n### Upper running text (continuing I:19; continues I:21)\n\nFor even a necessary matter may become the subject of disagreement, whether through obstinate refusal or inadequate understanding. Otherwise, the divine realities [the sentence is completed on the following page] …\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned (on «فإن الضروري قد يقع»)\n\nOne may choose to say that the judgment that investigation produces knowledge is itself theoretical, and that it is established by *a particular investigation*, without any resulting circle or contradiction. For example, when we say, “The world is changing; and everything changing is originated,” that, as is known, is a form of investigation. It produces knowledge that the world is originated. It follows that investigation is something that produces knowledge, as the Imām maintained. Something of this device is indicated in the author's marginal observation: it enables a response to someone who asserts the universal negative proposition [that investigation yields no knowledge whatever].\n\nNor does it follow that the two instances are the same in the respect at issue. The same thing can differ according to the consideration under which it is taken, and consequently differ in what can be predicated of it. Thus what is required is an investigation concerning investigation *as a means of knowledge*, not simply the performance of any operation called investigation.\n\nThere is, further, a distinction between what is assumed in the inference and what the inference establishes. In an investigation, the propositions that serve as premises must be known; their being known is presupposed in the inference. But the conclusion concerning the efficacy of investigation need not already have been assumed as a premise. Otherwise every demonstrated conclusion would be declared circular merely because its demonstration depends upon something already known.\n\nMore precisely, the disputed point is the particular property of an investigation by virtue of which it stands as an instrument leading to knowledge. The statement that a thing is known by virtue of evidence does not require the evidence and the thing established to be identically known in the same respect. [The original here moves through the terms *mawḍūʿ*, *makhṣūṣ* and the ‘subject’ of the judgment; the compressed syntactic chain still requires a second literal collation.]\n\nThe same point is illustrated through the familiar example of things called “originated.” We may say that the world is originated, or that every thing whose existence is preceded by nonexistence is originated; but the word may also be applied under a restricted description. From the use of one expression for these cases we cannot simply infer the very feature which is under dispute. In this way, the answer to the objection that a particular theoretical judgment must depend upon its own prior establishment is that the dependency is not in the same respect.\n\nThe glossator then returns to the distinction between a universal judgment and its instances: to have knowledge of one instance is not necessarily to possess the very universal conclusion whose possibility is disputed. Treating the particular and universal as identical would make knowledge of a particular member of a kind into knowledge of the whole kind without the required inference. [The last few compressed lines about the universality and particularity of the judgment and a subsequent lemma are left for exact re-reading; they are not counted as fully translated.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":21,"body":"— The teacher's authority and scepticism about perception\n**Physical PDF 34.**\n\n### Upper running text (continued from I:20; continues I:22)\n\n… cannot be conceived in their very quiddities. Even if it is conceded that they can, the conceptions in question stand in opposition [to the proposed alternative proof]. Furthermore, the guidance of a teacher does not benefit [us] except after his truthfulness is known. His truthfulness is either known through investigation—in which case investigation is sufficient for knowledge—or through that teacher's own statement, which is circular, since his statement that he is truthful cannot benefit us until his truthfulness has already been known. Or it is known through the word of another teacher, in which case a regress follows.\n\n**[Embedded lemma and reply.]** The one who rejects [the possibility of knowledge] through obstinacy is a stubborn opponent whose allegation is not heard. Likewise the Sophist who denies sensible truths, or first principles, or both, is a disputant whose claims are not admitted. The printed author glosses the name *Sophist*, explaining the Greek components as signifying knowledge and error, and presents the group as a class of philosophers. [The school-etymology is reported as printed and is not independently endorsed.]\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned (on «لا يفيد إلا بعد العلم بصدقه»)\n\nIt has been objected that we do not make the reception of knowledge depend upon an independently established recognition of the teacher's truthfulness. Rather, the point may be taken to be that the knowledge thereby conveyed is itself truthful or certain. But if the intended way of gaining knowledge is investigation, it remains necessary to distinguish a recognition gained through the instruments of inquiry from a recognition independently guaranteed by the teacher's report. The alleged teacher cannot provide precisely what he is being asked to establish simply by asserting that he provides it.\n\nThe marginal writer then questions the wider supposition that inquiry is indispensable to all cognition. The appeal to the need for inquiry in obtaining knowledge is liable to overlook instances in which someone comes to possess recognition without an explicit investigation. Likewise the claim that no such knowledge is possible has to contend with the many cases of persons already knowing what the sceptic sets out to deny. [Several small-type examples of persons and their states of knowledge are compressed and need independent reading; their identity is not supplied here.]\n\nA further consideration is that the senses in which a person is described as knowing must not be conflated. What is presented as someone's attainment of faith or recognition may not have involved a demonstrative inference of the sort now being discussed. A universal claim about the necessity of demonstration cannot simply be extracted from a case where a person first learned something from another's speech.\n\n### Lower commentary — separate note on «اسم طائفة من الحكماء» (begins here)\n\nThe name is given to a group of people who took pleasure in sophistical arguments. The writer reports that different schools among them were distinguished according to what they denied: some denied primary intellectual truths, some denied sense knowledge, and some challenged both. The distinctions are not all treated as one thesis; they prepare the upper text's separate refutations. [The printed historical anecdote and continuation at the foot of I:21 carry into I:22 and remain to be collated literally.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":22,"body":"— Attacks upon sense and upon first principles\n**Physical PDF 35.**\n\n### Upper running text\n\nAmong them are some who object to sensibles by saying that the judgment of sense sometimes goes wrong: a stationary thing may be seen as moving, and conversely, as one sees a ship as stationary when it is moving, while the shore appears to move though it is stationary. Hence, they argue, a judgment of sense in any particular case is liable to error and ought not to be accepted.\n\nThe answer is that immediate evidentness excludes the supposition of error in *certain* sensible matters. An error in some cases does not rule out conforming, certain judgment in many other cases: for example, in judging that the sun illuminates and that fire burns, and similar judgments.\n\nOthers attack the first principles, arguing that the most evident of them is our assertion that affirmation and negation do not occur together and do not both fail to occur: that is, a thing either is or is not. Yet, they say, even this is not beyond doubt, since knowing this proposition presupposes forming the conceptions of existence and nonexistence—being and not-being—[continued on I:23].\n\n### Lower commentary — first two small notes\n\n**On «والجواب أن البداهة»:** This means the immediacy of the *intellect's* recognition, not that of sense [invoked to prove itself]; otherwise the answer would be an attempt to establish the authority of sense through sense itself.\n\n**On «إلى غير ذلك»:** That is, other instances of propositions known with customary certainty. [The closing abbreviation does not independently identify the writer.]\n\n### Lower commentary — longer objection, unassigned\n\nThe investigators observe that there has scarcely been anyone among the learned who wholeheartedly adopted this position [of denying all sensible and primary truths]. Instead, it is often said to characterize extreme Sophists, whether through their mistaken conception or as a polemical designation. The marginal writer connects the name of the group with one who denies sensibles and then places the issue within the reports of particular schools and philosophers.\n\nOn the objection concerning sensible things: it is the proper starting point to confine the objection to the sensible judgments alone, rather than already to extend it to the whole of intellectual cognition. To claim that the sense faculty errs in certain judgments is not to have shown that all judgments of sensation fail. [The note further discusses the appearance of error and the possibility of attributing certain deceptive cases to another faculty's judgment; the precise assignment of the examples in the print requires a second pass.]\n\nOn the mention of *first principles*, the question is whether the supposedly self-evident propositions are themselves put under suspicion. Some have interpreted the denial of sensible things as secondary to a denial of fundamental judgments, since the correctness of certain sensory claims presupposes the mind's acceptance of an evident distinction. But the denial of one class does not of itself entail the denial of every other. [The following argument continues on I:23.]","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":23,"body":"— Can knowledge of noncontradiction depend upon noncontradiction?\n**Physical PDF 36.**\n\n### Upper running text\n\n… and upon grasping the meaning of a thing's being a subject and of its being a predicate. But the opponent says that understanding these notions inevitably depends upon the reality of the very proposition being disputed, since it is one of the first principles upon which all the others depend. For instance, when considering the proposition that the whole is greater than the part, we see that if the whole were not greater, the part would have to be greater than the whole; and that is impossible. It is alleged that an appeal of this kind already employs the principle of noncontradiction. Thus, if understanding the principle were made dependent upon that reasoning, circularity would result.\n\nThe reply is that the intellect judges this proposition decisively, without investigation or inferential proof. Those who attack both sensibles and first principles have thereby rejected what is immediately evident, and their objection does not defeat the original judgment simply by calling it into question. [The closing compressed clause about the opponent's admission and the kinds of evidence requires a second literal check.]\n\n### Lower commentary — continuation and separated lemmatic notes\n\n**On «وعلى تحقق معنى كون الشيء موضوعا»:** What is meant is the intellectual grasp of a thing as *subject*, and of something else as *predicate*. But it is open to objection whether this understanding itself needs the judgment whose priority is being alleged.\n\n**On «توقف لا محالة»:** It is better to construe the clause as the answer to the hypothetical condition, rather than as an attribute describing investigation. Otherwise it might misleadingly suggest that there is some investigation which need not terminate in the relevant first principle. On this construal, the consequent of the supposition is the following statement, “Then circularity follows.”\n\n**On «قوله لازمة بها»:** This refers to the proposition, that is, to the predicative relation it contains. The writer's point is that judging affirmatively or negatively presupposes the ability to distinguish the two. This does not prove that every explicit piece of reasoning about that distinction must precede the immediate understanding of it.\n\n**On «من غير نظر واستدلال»:** That is, without making judgment of the first principle depend on a further investigation. From the fact that assent to the first principle is immediately given it does not follow that the *conceptual terms* involved in a later demonstration must each have been established through it as a prior conclusion. On this account the apparent circle is removed by distinguishing direct grasp from the inferential use of a prior judgment.\n\nThe gloss also suggests construing the phrase about “first principles” with reference to the immediacy of the subject-predicate judgment. A reader should not take the text to mean that *every* concept preceding assent is already an inferential conclusion. The qualification is required to keep the case of direct conceptual apprehension distinct from the later deduction. [The final several lines, discussing ‘direction of relation’ and responses to the denial of first principles, remain provisionally rendered and are not fully diplomatically established.]\n\n---\n\n# Source-checked continuation I:24–33 (still requires a second read)","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":24,"body":"— Is knowledge after investigation a matter of customary conjunction or generation?\n\n### Larger upper text (the running argument)\n\n[What has been discussed] earlier, in reply and argument. **“And is it”**—that is, the investigation's being productive of knowledge—**“by way of custom”**? This is on the assumption that all possible existents are referred to God, exalted is He, directly and from the outset, and that He is able to choose. Thus nothing must proceed from Him of necessity. **“Or”** is it **“by way of generation”**, on the assumption that [not] everything is attributed to Him immediately? [The words about direct and mediated action are continued on the next original page.]\n\n### Lower text, first printed gloss: «قال المصنف رحمه الله بطريق العادة»\n\nThe author, God have mercy on him, said, **“By way of custom.”** This is the Ashʿarī position: knowledge of the conclusion occurs following correct investigation through the operation of custom. That is based upon the principle that all possible things, in their entirety, are referred to God directly, and that no one of them has an independent part in bringing another into existence. Rather, God may bring some things into existence after others, without their being necessitated by Him, for He is a choosing agent. If He repeatedly brings one after another into existence, that is called **custom**; if it does not recur, it is called a departure from custom. Knowledge obtained after investigation is, without doubt, a possible and recurrent event. Thus it is referred to custom. [The note ends with a conventional pious closing.] \n\n### Lower text, second printed gloss: «قال رحمه الله أو التوليد»\n\nHe said, God have mercy on him, **“Or generation.”** This is what the Muʿtazilites hold. It follows from their principle that our voluntary acts proceed from us: either **directly**, when they do not proceed from us through another of our own acts, or **by generation**, when they do proceed through another act of ours. Accordingly they claim that the knowledge occurring after investigation is an act proceeding from us through the mediation of investigation; thus its proceeding from us occurs by generation. [The printed note ends with a pious closing.]\n\n### Lower text, further short lemmatic notes\n\n**On “by way of custom”:** That is, through the fact that this regularly occurs, while its failing to occur remains possible as a departure from custom.\n\n**On “so nothing is necessitated by Him”:** Because, as will be stated, the effect of a choosing agent is not necessary. Those who hold this position include people who assign it exclusively to divine power, without the servant's power attaching to it, and others who make it an acquisition (*kasb*) which is also within the servant's power.\n\n**On “or generation”:** On their position, the knowledge obtained from investigation is among the servant's acts and among the things within the servant's power; so is investigation itself. However, the occurrence of the first act is followed by the second. It is reported that some of our associates, after rejecting generation generally, argued against it here too: if investigation generated knowledge, recollecting the investigation would generate knowledge just as initiating it does, since there is no relevant difference. This is answered by saying that the argument does not produce certainty, since it returns to an analogy of a disputed sort, and a difference between the two cases is apparent. [The last words describing the analogy's kind and its answer should be checked at greater magnification.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":25,"body":"— Direct production, *tawlīd*, and emanation by necessity\n\n### Larger upper text (beginning within the I:24 sentence)\n\n[For] He, exalted is He, is an agent who acts directly. The meaning of **generation** is that an act issues from an agent **through another act issuing from him**, as with the hand's movement [causing] the key's movement: the key's movement issues by reason of the hand's movement. Its opposite is **direct action**, which is the issuance of an act from him without an intermediary. It is objected that knowledge does not belong to the category of *action*. The response is that **the other act** in this case is *imparting knowledge* (*ifādat al-ʿilm*), which is an action. **“Or”** it occurs **“by way of necessity,”** on the assumption that the effusion of originated things from the overflowing principle takes place of necessity when the receiving subject is completely prepared. [The criticism of this theory begins on I:26.]\n\n### Lower text, first printed gloss: «قال قدس سره أو الوجوب»\n\nHe said, may his spirit be sanctified, **“Or necessity.”** This is the view of the philosophers. It is based on their principle that the overflowing principle, with respect to the existence of originated things, necessitates [its effects] by essence, although their effusion from it depends upon the presence of complete preparedness. It is known that the knowledge arising after investigation is an originated thing, and so it is included under that rule. The author has omitted a **fourth** position: it is the view chosen by Imām al-Rāzī, al-Ghazālī and the Imām of the Two Sanctuaries. Its purport is that the occurrence of knowledge after sound investigation is necessary by a rational necessity, while it is not generated from the investigation. [The note closes with a prayer.]\n\n### Lower text, second printed gloss: «قوله واعترض بأن العلم»\n\n**On “and it was objected that knowledge ...”:** The *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* says, in substance, that investigation itself, on the more precise view, is not an item in the category of action either. But by **action** the disputants intended what is produced through an agent, not the agent's influencing as such. They have indeed agreed that the motion of the hand and the motion of the key are [ascribed to] the agent, although motion is not, strictly speaking, a member of the category of action.\n\n**On “the other act here is imparting knowledge”:** I say that this “other act” cannot simply be assumed to be the generated act of the servant. Even if imparting knowledge is granted to be an action, the question remains: is it the action of the one performing investigation, or [what is described as] the action of the investigation? The distinction matters for whether this constitutes generation in the technical sense. [The source's compressed objection here is not yet textually secure in every word.]\n\n**On “from the overflowing principle”:** They intend by this the **Active Intellect**, which they describe as impressed with the forms of the generated things, the forms overflowing from it upon our souls according to their preparedness. They maintained that **the Spirit** and **the Clear Book** in the language of revelation are expressions referring to it. This is reported in the commentary on the *Maqāṣid*. [The historical attribution is translated, not endorsed.]\n\n**On the last distinction:** [A final short objection about whether the alleged generation is truly established and whether one can validly infer the later event merely from an earlier occurrence follows. Its tightly compressed printed syntax needs an independent check. It has **not** been replaced by an invented completion.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":26,"body":"— The fourth position and the difference between rational entailment and productive causation\n\n### Larger upper text\n\nThis [account of necessity] is built on the denial of God's freedom of choice—exalted is He exceedingly above that. **“There is disagreement about it”** among the Ashʿarites, the Muʿtazilites and the philosophers, in the order already mentioned. **“And investigation for knowledge of God, exalted is He”**—that is, for the sake of obtaining certainty [the predication “is obligatory” is printed at the start of I:27].\n\n### Lower text, separate short gloss: «قوله لأجل حصول اليقين»\n\n**On “for the sake of obtaining certainty”:** This indicates that, in the explanation, *knowledge* and *recognition* mean **certain assent**, because knowledge concerning a particular object may occur by sensing it, which itself entails certain assent to its existence. [The printed note ends with the customary abbreviation.]\n\n### Lower text, second long gloss: «قوله فيه خلاف بين الأشاعرة»\n\n**On “there is disagreement among the Ashʿarites”:** He has mentioned that there is here another position, chosen by Imām al-Rāzī. The Proof of Islam [al-Ghazālī] states that it is the position of the majority of our associates. It is that **investigation entails knowledge of the conclusion by way of necessity**, without investigation being a cause of it as in the philosophers' doctrine, or a generating act as in the Muʿtazilite doctrine.\n\nBut the assertion of necessity must be made clear. If what is meant is merely that the knowledge usually follows the investigation, then the account reverts to the first [Ashʿarī] doctrine. What is intended is **rational entailment**: one who knows the investigation [as an inference] cannot fail to know the conclusion, just as is the case with necessary implications generally.\n\nThe meaning is not, however, that God ceases to be a choosing agent. If what is meant is that God could refrain from bringing about the knowledge by not attaching His power and choice to it, then the account becomes the first doctrine again. Nor does the necessitation described here necessarily contradict the knowledge's being an effect of a powerful and choosing agent, who is able to act and to refrain. For **refraining** can mean His not bringing about the act, without requiring that, while He produces the entire cause with all its conditions, He also produces an effect apart from it.\n\nThe possibility of refraining is wider than the absence of every intermediary. Consequently, the occurrence may be conditioned upon the absence of an impediment; and that condition too may be within divine power. The source draws a comparison here with products of generated actions in the account of the Muʿtazilites, who assign them to the servant's power. What contradicts the possibility of leaving an action undone is **necessity in the sense of the absolute impossibility of separation from the effective cause**. It is this last sense, rather than every sense of rational entailment, that is under dispute.\n\nAccordingly, on this fourth account the entailment of theoretical knowledge is **rational**, without this in itself denying the choice of the agent. On the first account the connection is **customary**, in the sense explained above. [The note closes at the foot of I:26; the detailed interpretation of one conditional connective in the middle requires a second grammatical collation.]\n\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":27,"body":"— Whether the prerequisite of an absolute obligation is obligatory\n\n### Larger upper text (one very short line, completing I:26)\n\n**“... is obligatory for [establishing] His existence.”** [The subject is rational investigation for knowledge of God, introduced on I:26; the printed upper line occupies only the top of this leaf. Everything after the rule is lower commentary, not Sanandajī's main text.]\n\n### Lower commentary — one long argument, author unassigned\n\n**On «واجب».** That is, [investigation is] obligatory by revelation if the obligation of the unconditionally obligatory act is established by revelation, and by reason if it is established by reason, as in the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*.\n\nKnow that there is disagreement over this well-known premise, and confusion in the ways of setting it out. Most writers hold that the **prerequisite within one's power, upon which an absolute obligation depends, is also obligatory** by reason of the obligation of that act—absolutely, whether it is a *cause* or a *condition*. They argue in one fashion that, if the prerequisite were not obligatory, leaving aside what the obligation depends upon would be permitted, and the originally obligatory act would consequently not be obligatory. They argue in another that, if the prerequisite of an unconditioned obligation were not obligatory, one could lawfully leave it undone while the obligation of the primary act continued. Yet the act without its prerequisite is impossible. Obliging the person to perform it in that condition would therefore be impossible.\n\nOthers maintain that the prerequisite does **not** inherit the obligation in an absolute way. Against them it is said that the indication obliging the act is silent concerning the obligation of its prerequisite; and some qualify the principle by saying that the prerequisite is obligatory if it is a *cause*, but not if it is a *condition*, on the ground that the cause has a closer connection with the effect than the condition has with what is conditioned.\n\nAnother group argues that where the thing upon which an obligatory act depends is a condition, the condition must be treated differently: the lawgiver has not necessarily made the condition an object of the command merely by commanding the conditioned act. One must first decide whether the presence of the condition itself was required. The absence of a condition can mean that the obligation has not become operative rather than that a presently binding obligation cannot be performed.\n\nThe question is not exhausted by contrasting causes and conditions. A cause too may stand in a relation to the effect that is different from the relation of a means one is able to provide. The intended prerequisite is one by whose procurement a presently binding act can be performed. What is entirely outside the agent's power does not fall under the requirement to procure it in the same way. Much of the controversy concerns precisely how the parties define the *prerequisite* and the *unconditional obligation*.\n\nIt is also necessary to distinguish what is *obligatory in itself* from what becomes obligatory by virtue of another obligation. When the command is directed to an end, an indispensable means may be required **for that end**, without being intended as an independent end for which the lawgiver has issued a second, separately worded command. In that case, the question whether omitting the means amounts to a distinct culpable omission has to be settled rather than assumed.\n\nIf some writers describe an absolute obligation as obligatory notwithstanding the absence of its prerequisite, while others say that an impossible thing cannot be commanded, this should not immediately be taken as a contradiction. One may mean that the obligation is in force and the person must procure the means, while the other means that performance without the means is impossible. The two assertions differ in their object.\n\nThere remains a difficulty with the alleged proof from silence. The text indicating that an act is obligatory may say nothing expressly about its prerequisite. Yet silence as to an express *separate* obligation does not prove that the prerequisite need not be procured. Conversely, merely pointing out that the end was commanded is not, without more, an explicit command to perform every particular means that might contribute to it.\n\nThe glossator returns to the common interpretation that an unconditioned obligation requires its indispensable means when these lie in the agent's power. He notes that many of the verbal disagreements in the books are better explained by distinguishing the ways in which the obligation of the prerequisite is meant, rather than declaring the familiar premise true or false without qualification. The final portion of his comparison with al-*Maqāṣid* and some later theologians runs on to I:28. [One passage about the exact distinction between **cause** (*sabab*) and **condition** (*sharṭ*) remains difficult, and this paragraph is an interpretive translation of its argument rather than a verified diplomatic rendering.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":28,"body":"— Revealed proof and the prerequisite of obligatory knowledge\n\n### Larger upper text (continuing the line on I:27)\n\n**“By the text”**—that is, the Book and the Sunna—**“and by consensus,”** namely the community's agreement on its obligation; **“and because it”**—namely rational investigation—**“is within one's power, and a prerequisite of the obligatory knowledge”** in the case under discussion.\n\n### Lower commentary — continuation of the long I:27 argument\n\n[The writer continues comparing the earlier explanations.] These explanations do not make the statement of someone denying the obligation stand as the straightforward contradictor of the other statement, although the disputants have treated them as contradictories—as is plain to anyone who examines the books. Further, the explanation offered for the position associated with Imām al-Ḥaramayn indicates that by this premise they mean the obligation of a prerequisite **which the Lawgiver requires the person to obtain**, not merely every item without which the commanded thing cannot exist under any possible description.\n\nThe material already cited contains more than one meaning of the *absolutely obligatory*. Some treatments make it a legal question specifically, while others formulate the rule in general terms. The more evident interpretation is that **the act's being obligatory does not automatically imply a separately stated obligation of its prerequisite**. The claim that this would require commanding the impossible is answered by distinguishing two things. What is impossible is to command the performance of a thing **without the existence of what it depends upon**. What actually follows from the disputed position is only that the act may be commanded **without an explicit declaration that its prerequisite is obligatory**. This is not impossible: silence about the prerequisite at the time the primary act is made obligatory does not establish that the prerequisite is not obligatory.\n\nFurthermore, the obligation of the act entails the obligation of the *available prerequisite*, considered as a prerequisite—that is, something upon which the thing's existence depends, whether rationally or in another respect. This is the sense in which the argument should be considered. [Here the writer calls attention to his own way of resolving the dispute.]\n\n**On «أمراً مقدوراً».** The word “within one's power” is broader than an *act* directly subject to power. Something may be possible for an agent to procure even if it is not itself an act of the same kind, since the agent's power may attach to bringing it about or to its causes. For that reason the wording says **“a thing”** (*amr*), rather than simply **“an act”** (*fiʿl*).\n\n**On «للمعرفة الواجبة».** It has been objected that knowledge's being obligatory depends on the possibility of *commanding* knowledge, and this possibility is disputed. If a person already has the knowledge, commanding him to acquire it would be demanding what is already present; if he has none, the demand seems to be addressed to someone unaware of what is demanded. But the response is that its being commandable is immediately evident once the relevant distinction is made. A person to whom the summons has not reached, or who has received the words but does not comprehend their meaning, is not the same as a legally accountable person who has understood the demand but does not yet know its object. Such a person can be charged with coming to know it. [The source's final phrase distinguishes the addressee's awareness of the **command** from possession of the **knowledge commanded**; its exact last few words need a second reading.]\n\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":29,"body":"— Does the command to know God imply an unconditioned obligation to investigate?\n\n### Upper register (one printed line, continuing I:28)\n\n… that is, by text and consensus, and [the prerequisite] which lies within one's power, which is a prerequisite on which the unconditionally obligatory act depends.\n\n### Lower text: brief lemma on «الواجب المطلق»\n\nBy “the unconditionally obligatory” is meant an act whose obligation is *not* conditioned on the thing upon which its performance depends—as the obligation of almsgiving, for example, is conditioned on the possession of the minimum amount (*niṣāb*). [That comparison explains why not every prerequisite must be procured under an already binding command.]\n\n### Lower text: objection and reply on «أي بالنص والإجماع»\n\nIt has been denied that a proof has been established for the obligation of gaining knowledge through investigation. As for **scriptural text**, one cannot simply cite the Almighty's words, “Know that there is no deity but God,” because the indication is not decisive: a command may be used for something other than obligation. As for **consensus**, its evidentiary force is questioned in this matter; the opponent instead maintains that **following authority** (*taqlīd*) suffices. For the Companions, may God be pleased with them, accepted Islam from people who followed them without imposing upon every one of them the requirement of critical verification and inferential demonstration.\n\nIt has been answered that the question concerns the obligation of investigation according to revealed law, and that accepting an outward profession of Islam does not establish that the person's inward recognition is based solely on unreasoning imitation. The source discusses the possibility that knowledge is reached by proofs whose possession is not formally expressed as an argument. The words “not demanding a demonstration” must therefore not be made equivalent to “declaring demonstration forbidden or unnecessary in every case.” [Two clauses about transmitted versus inferential evidence remain to be checked in the lower printed text.]\n\n### Lower text: a second explanation of «الواجب المطلق»\n\nThe *unconditionally obligatory* does not mean what is obligatory under **all imaginable supposition and states**. Otherwise no obligatory act at all would be unconditionally obligatory, for every obligation is qualified by certain conditions—including the agent's not being incapable of it. What is intended is rather that, once the prescribed obligation is present, its being obligatory is not conditioned on the presence of **the prerequisite under consideration**. Thus the obligation of an act can remain operative even when the means to perform it has not yet been provided.\n\nIt does not follow that all things whose absence would render performance impossible are therefore separately enjoined as independent duties. But where the prerequisite is within the agent's power, neglecting it can frustrate a duty already binding. The example of almsgiving is again instructive: obtaining the minimum property threshold is not commanded merely on the ground that almsgiving becomes obligatory when the threshold exists, whereas seeking water for an obligation of purification already binding may stand differently.\n\nAccordingly, the conclusion “investigation is obligatory because it is the prerequisite of obligatory knowledge” must show that the *knowledge* is already binding on the addressee and that inquiry is the required available route to it. If the knowledge were not obligatory, its means would not acquire obligation merely from being a means. [The gloss closes by promising a further discussion of the difference between the obligation of a prerequisite and the condition of an obligation.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":30,"body":"— The Muʿtazilite argument from fear of punishment\n\n### Upper register\n\n**Among the Muʿtazilites**, investigation directed to knowledge of God is obligatory, because it is a prerequisite of the knowledge that is obligatory, **since knowledge averts a feared harm**, namely the apprehension of punishment. On our account the obligation is established by the **revealed law**; on theirs it is **rational**.\n\n**They say:** If the obligation of investigation depended on a command of the revealed law—such as the Prophet's, peace be upon him, call to consider the miracle, and in general whatever must be investigated before his truthfulness can be known—then [the existence and authority of] the law would have to be established first. Yet establishing [the authority of] that law depends on investigation into the miracle and the signs of prophecy. Consequently, it would not be right to say that one must investigate the miracle only after the law's authoritative command had been established. That would be circular.\n\n**We say in reply:** Investigation is not obligatory prior to the establishment of the law **by virtue of a legal command**. The fact that one may have to investigate the signs of prophecy before accepting the law as authoritative does not establish that the legal obligation to do so already existed at that earlier stage. [The end of the upper argument and its reply continue into I:31.]\n\n### Lower note on «خوف العقاب»\n\nThat is, fear of punishment on account of *leaving* the recognition of God, exalted is He, and of the harm expected from doing so. If the fearful expectation is removed by the attainment of knowledge, then the means leading to knowledge is said to be obligatory under the rational principle of avoiding probable harm. [The note does not itself establish that the feared harm necessarily occurs.]\n\n### Lower note on «وفي جميع ما يتوقف ثبوته على ثبوته»\n\nThat is, in everything whose establishment depends upon the establishment of the revelation's authority. The law's being authoritative is itself dependent upon establishing the truthfulness of the Lawgiver's messenger. In turn the proof of the messenger's truthfulness rests upon knowledge of the miracle and what makes it a sign. These steps are themselves matters of investigation. If the legal obligation of that very investigation were claimed to depend upon the already established law, the difficulty would recur.\n\nThe glossator notes the difference between asserting that something **cannot be known without** a prior proof and asserting that there must already be a **legal command to perform** that proof. A rationally intelligible inference about which knowledge comes first is not yet a demonstration that religious law imposed an obligation before its own authority was acknowledged. [The last small-type lines develop the rejoinder that a revealed obligation of inquiry may arise only once revelation is established; the precise syntactic relation of one clause remains uncertain.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":31,"body":"— How could revelation command the investigation which authenticates it?\n\n### Upper register\n\nThe revealed law is not established for one who has not investigated, because its proof is itself theoretical. **It is answered**, first, that the fact that [the law's authority] is not yet known to a certain person does not entail the impossibility of a rational act of investigation. The opponent's inference from lack of established legal obligation to lack of all access to knowledge is not valid.\n\nSecondly, the obligation of investigation in the first instance does not require that the law already have been accepted by the very addressee as a conclusion. Its evidential basis can be established through another route before he knows it. [The phrasing across the first few lines is compressed, and the complete shape of the first answer should be collated from I:30–31.]\n\nThe opponent also claims that, because the obligation of the principal knowledge is rational, investigation must be obligatory. But one must distinguish the **knowledge which is sought** from the **investigation which leads to it**. The former may be obligatory, while the latter is the way to reach that obligation; the same inferential relation does not prove that every legal command's authority exists before the demonstration which establishes it.\n\nOn the obligation of investigation, the text then places before the reader another inquiry: **“What is the first obligation?”** Some have said it is **knowledge [of God]**. Some have said it is **investigation**, as the means of knowledge. Others have said it is the **intention to investigate**, because intention precedes investigation. The distinction is between what is first *as the sought end* and what is first *in the order of performing what leads to the end*. [The next printed page resumes the dispute about intention.]\n\n### Lower short lemma on «بأن المتوقف على ثبوته»\n\nThere is an objection to the assertion that the law depends upon inquiry: on the account already given, establishing the authority of revelation is not the same as establishing an already binding legal obligation to investigate. The author of the note again distinguishes a proof's epistemic priority from a command's binding force.\n\n### Lower gloss on «ولا يثبت الشرع عند من لم ينظر»\n\nThe sense of the objection is that if someone has not yet investigated, revelation's authority is not established *for that person*, so how can he be charged by it with performing the very inquiry that he has not yet performed? The answer appeals to the possibility that the Lawgiver's demand be known or made intelligible prior to the full inferential recognition, and it warns against assuming that every way of understanding a command requires completing the same theological demonstration.\n\nThe alleged circularity can therefore be dissolved by distinguishing the person who has heard and understood an invitation to investigate from the person who has already affirmed the Prophet's authority through a completed proof. The invitation can be understood as a demand before the addressee has the final assent whose acquisition is its object. [The writer then qualifies this on the ground that a command's being binding is not simply the same as its being intelligible; the remaining printed clause on this leaf requires exact collation.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":32,"body":"— The first duty: knowledge, inquiry, or intending to inquire?\n\n### Upper register\n\nThe scholars who maintain the obligation of knowledge and of inquiry have differed concerning the **first duty**. Some state that the first duty is knowledge itself, and others that it is investigation, because it is the condition of reaching knowledge. Others say that it is **the intention to undertake investigation**: the act of inquiry cannot be performed intentionally unless one first directs oneself toward it. But this proposal has to be distinguished from an endless regress of intentions before intentions.\n\nThe author then gives the evidential basis for considering a question of this kind. **“The proof”** is that [the matter about which inquiry is commanded] must admit of cognition, and the investigation must lead toward a **decisive judgment** (*ḥukm jāzim*) or toward its contrary; the discussion also mentions **guesswork** or **probability** (*ẓann*) and whether a demanded assent is grounded in sound evidence.\n\nIt is indicated that what is properly demanded in theological inquiry is a **decisive judgment conforming to reality** (*al-jāzim fī muqābalat al-amāra* [printed phrase to be rechecked]). If what was called knowledge were merely a thought appearing likely, one would not yet have what the dispute calls certain knowledge.\n\nThe possible sources of evidence are then divided into intellectual and transmitted: **reason** and the **true report**, with the indispensable intellectual principles that make the employment of a transmitted proof possible. One cannot dispense entirely with the reasoning required to understand what revelation reports, even when revelation itself is the authoritative testimony.\n\n### Lower notes — source reading, not yet a complete lower-text translation\n\n**On «بل القصد»:** The intention to investigate is proposed as the first duty because the performance of inquiry rests upon that intention. The text distinguishes an unending regress of intentions from the first determination to enter an act of investigation; one need not suppose that every intention requires another prior intention.\n\n**On the alleged obligation of the means:** Some criticisms say that the premise concerning the prerequisite of a duty only establishes that investigation is necessary *after* the obligation of knowledge is established. On the other hand, some opponents deny that investigation is always necessary for knowledge, since one may reach knowledge through a different route. The relation between the legal command and epistemic priority must therefore be demonstrated, not simply stipulated.\n\n**On intending to investigate:** The gloss questions whether the intention is obligatory as a separate act or whether an already willed act of investigation includes the requisite intention. It warns against arguing in a circle: knowledge's being obligatory is invoked to establish investigation's obligation, and investigation's obligation to establish the obligation of its preceding intention. [The rest of the lower argument crosses the physical boundary into I:33 and requires separate segmentation; several lines are not transcribed or translated here.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":33,"body":"— Authentic opening of Book Two: general matters\n\n### Upper register: closing the preceding discussion\n\n[The text concludes the treatment of] beliefs and the establishing of other discussions that depend upon the beliefs. Accordingly, it is proper now to enter upon the intended theological subjects. We say:\n\n### Authentic printed division\n\n**BOOK TWO — ON GENERAL MATTERS**  \n**الباب الثاني في الأمور العامة**\n\n### Upper register: beginning of Book Two\n\nGeneral matters are those **which encompass all existents**, including the Necessary Being, substance and accident, such as **existence** and **unity**. For every existent, even if it is many, is one in some respect. [Other general considerations apply to] most existents, such as **specific possibility** (*al-imkān al-khāṣṣ*), **temporal origination**, **necessity through another**, **multiplicity**, and **being an effect**. The general matters are placed before the remaining subjects because they serve as principles for them. The investigation of existence is placed first because of its nobility. Hence the author says: **“The conception of existence ...”** [continued at I:34].\n\n### Lower gloss: why a book of general matters is needed\n\nThe gloss explains that, because matters peculiar to the Necessary Being, to substance and to accident are given their own places, a separate book is needed for **conditions shared** among them: either among all three, such as existence and [a certain general use of] causality, or among two, such as quiddity and causedness. Nonexistence is studied because it stands opposed to existence; impossibility because it pertains to nonexistence; and necessity and beginninglessness because they are conditions of existence. [Some items in the printed list and the note's concluding connective require further collation; no additional divisions are supplied from elsewhere.]\n\n### Lower commentary: what kind of preliminary knowledge is required?\n\nThe marginal writer resumes a qualification of the preceding logical account. When the purpose is **decisive knowledge**, the matters on which it depends must be established accordingly. But when what is sought is **only probable judgment**, the statement of one person or of several people whom the investigator considers truthful may suffice. If the knowledge derived from mass-transmission (*tawātur*) is treated as theoretical and inferential, as some maintain, even that should not be confused with the particular rational demonstrations required to establish God's existence and the truthfulness of prophets. These must be considered under their own conditions of certainty.\n\n**On “the Necessary, substance and accident”:** The gloss clarifies that “existents” here denotes the great divisions of what exists, not an assertion that every property belongs equally to every member of every division. [The printed lower discussion continues on I:34, where its exact scope can be fixed.]\n\n---\n\n---\n\n# NEW SOURCE-CHECKED CONTINUATION: I:34–44\n\n**Direct source-to-English working translation, 6 November 2026.** Original scan: `taqribmaram(2).pdf`, printed I:34–38 = physical PDF 47–51. The dividing rules in the original distinguish **the upper register** (Taftāzānī's parenthesised lemmata within Sanandajī's commentary) from **the printed lower registers**, whose individual writers are not identified by the layout. Text in square brackets is editorial supply or a notice that exact wording remains unresolved; it must not be mistaken for original Arabic. This is a continuing working translation, not a critical edition.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":34,"body":"— Is the conception of existence self-evident?\n\n**Upper register — first-pass translation of the printed sequence**\n\n> **“Necessary [or self-evident].”** For our assertion, “A thing is either existent or nonexistent,” is a self-evident assent; and that assent depends upon conceiving *the existent* and *the nonexistent*. The conceptions of both must, accordingly, be self-evident.\n>\n> **Objection:** But if you claim that the conceptions of all the constituents of this judgment are self-evident, that does not follow. For *existence* is one of its constituents; and even if you claim that the judgment here is necessary only *after* both terms have been conceived, that does not exclude either one of them—namely, existence—from being acquired through investigation rather than being self-evident. [The end of this sentence continues at the upper edge of I:35.]\n\n**Selected original wording:** «فإن قولنا الشيء إما موجود أو معدوم تصديق بديهي»; «وأنه يتوقف على تصور الموجود والمعدوم»; «وإن زعمت أن الحكم في هذا التصديق ...». The exact grammar of the compressed last upper sentence requires reinspection together with I:35.\n\n**Lower register — continuing gloss from I:33, unassigned writer; closely translated source clauses**\n\n> One might object: The discussion in this book has also concerned things that do not apply to an existent at all, such as **impossibility**, and things particular to the Necessary, such as pre-eternity and necessity. It is answered that the investigation of the former is incidental, because it stands opposite possibility; whereas the latter two concern divisions of necessity and of that which is not preceded by nonexistence. Being necessary either through itself or through another, and not being preceded by nonexistence, belong to the general matters. As for necessity, that is clear. As for pre-eternity, it is likewise so according to the philosophers who maintain that certain substances and accidents are beginningless. According to the mutakallimūn, however, it is not [general] in that sense, for their inquiry extends to whether the predicate is to be affirmed or denied. Reflect on this.\n>\n> On **“and it depends on conceiving the existent”**: If what is intended is dependence on conceiving [existence] *in its reality*, the premise is denied. If what is intended is dependence on conceiving it *in some sense or other*, it does not establish the required conclusion, namely that the conception of existence in its reality is self-evident.\n>\n> On **“thus it is self-evident”**: [A marginal authority, whose name is compressed here,] writes: One might say that this proves the self-evidence of conceiving the **derived expression** (*al-mushtaqq*), not the **source from which it is derived** (*al-maʾkhadh*), which is what we are discussing. The answer is: what is intended in the assent mentioned is the source from which the expression is derived, not the additional meaning of the derived expression. Accordingly, if [the relevant conception] is self-evident, that conception, too, is self-evident. [The reading of this annotation’s signature is unverified.]\n>\n> On **“that would beg the question”**: The point is that by *assent* here one may mean either, according to the Imām’s view, something **composite**, or, according to the philosopher's view, **the judgment itself**. On the first interpretation, either the self-evidence of all its parts is intended—in which case there is a begging of the question—or the self-evidence of only some of its parts, or of it without further specification, is intended—in which case the claimed conclusion does not follow. On the second interpretation, either the self-evidence of the judgment alone or its self-evidence without specification is intended, which likewise does not establish the conclusion; or the self-evidence of the judgment **together with all its objects of reference** (*mutaʿallaqāt*) is intended, in which case the argument begs the question again. It would be better, therefore, to speak of its **objects of reference** rather than its **parts**, as in the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*. Reflect.\n\n**Reading status:** This lower register has been checked sentence by sentence against the visible scan, but some small-type authorial markers and the disputed use of *qidam* remain uncertain. It is a **provisional source translation**, not a certified diplomatic transcription. The marginal writer remains unnamed.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":35,"body":"— Collective self-evidence and the self-evidence of the constituents\n\n**Upper register — continuation from I:34**\n\n> [The previous question continues:] It may be acquired, and yet the *assent* is self-evident without qualification, and there is no begging of the question; for its being self-evident without qualification, considered in the thing itself, does not mean that our knowledge of its self-evidence is independent of all the preliminary conceptions. [This sentence continues at the head of I:36, which supplies the explicit qualification.]\n\n**Lower register — long continuous objection and response; source-led translation**\n\n> On **“this assent is self-evident without qualification”**: That is, with all its parts, as stated in the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*. On **“and there is no begging of the question”**: The substance is that we choose the position that this judgment is self-evident together with all its related terms. There is no begging of the question, because although the self-evidence of the whole depends upon the self-evidence of each individual part, the purpose here is **not** to infer the self-evidence of the whole from the self-evidence of that part, which would indeed beg the question. Rather, the purpose is to infer, from our **knowledge that the whole is self-evident**, that the **part is self-evident**. This does not beg the question, for knowledge of the self-evidence of the whole does not depend upon knowledge of the self-evidence of its parts; it leads to that latter knowledge. The second may thus be derived from the first: when one knows that the whole is self-evident, then whichever of its parts one attends to, one knows that it is self-evident.\n>\n> This is the substance of what the author of the *Mawāqif* established. The author of the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* objected to it. The substance of his objection is this: knowledge of the whole is either **the same as knowledge of the parts** or **obtained through them**. It is therefore necessary that knowledge of the whole not be the prior source from which knowledge of a part is obtained. If it is said that one can apprehend a composite without attending to its parts in detail, we answer: this might be granted with a **real composite**, but not with a merely **notional composite**. To apprehend a notional composite is nothing but to apprehend the several items for which a single name has been established.\n>\n> Even if that were granted regarding *conception*, it would not be granted in the case of *assent*: there is no meaning to affirming that a composite is self-evident in all its parts other than affirming that this part of it is self-evident, and that part is self-evident, and so on. Even if this were granted, moreover, the feared begging of the question would not follow in every formulation. A proof may be known generally without one already possessing separate knowledge of the particular constituent that is identical with the conclusion. [This last concession is compressed in the print.]\n>\n> **I say:** The inference from the self-evidence of the composite under discussion to the self-evidence of its constituent **existence** resolves into the following: “Existence is a part of this composite; every part of this composite is self-evident.” This is a syllogism in the **first figure**. Once its premises are conceded, it yields decisively that existence is self-evident. There is therefore no objection at all [on that supposition]. It may be that the author intended, by his objection, to point instead to the difficulty set out in the writings of the earlier scholars about the first figure. If so, the criticism is not particular to this place: it would apply to any form of argument which seeks to establish a property of a part from a property of the whole. And even if all parts were held to be evident, what prevents the conclusion from being derived in this way?\n>\n> [The final words turn to a different objection: whether the evident *conception of existence* has been assumed in the very proof that seeks to establish it. The sentence continues on I:36.]\n\n**Reading status:** All principal objections and responses on this leaf have been translated as an argument, not replaced with a topical digest. A few compressed connectives and the exact reference to the earlier logical objection remain for a subsequent literal collation. The voice remains a **lower-register writer of unconfirmed identity**.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":36,"body":"— Immediacy of assent; the word and the reality defined\n\n**Upper register — continuation from I:35**\n\n> [The self-evidence of the judgment] in itself depends upon the self-evidence of its constituents *in the thing itself*. But our knowledge that the judgment is absolutely self-evident does not depend upon our knowing that every one of its constituents is self-evident. Otherwise the conclusion would be obtained only by first conceding the very point that is to be proved. One must therefore distinguish the **actual necessity of the assent** from **the way we recognise its necessity**.\n>\n> **“And definition”:** He means by it an explanation of the meaning indicated by the expression, not the disclosure of an unknown essence through a real definition. Thus the explanation “by *being* or *occurrence* or *realisation*,” in terms of verbal usage, is something different from a real definition that would make the quiddity known. [The subsequent words continue the distinction of definition and verbal explanation.]\n\n**Separate short marginal note, upper of two lower blocks**\n\n> On **“knowledge of the whole does not depend …”**: Knowledge may be either summary or detailed. Obtaining the former does not depend upon obtaining the latter. Accordingly, it is permissible to regard the obtaining of the knowledge of the whole as evidence for the self-evidence of those components through which the conception of existence is acquired. [The end of the note is compressed.]\n\n**Long lower note — partial source reading of the continuation from I:34–35, unassigned writer; NOT a complete literal translation**\n\n> The conception of the existence of a particular thing is indeed necessary. But it is even more apparent that [certain inferential claims] do not establish that the *general conception of existence* is necessary without qualification. The argument being used here is more limited than its apparent conclusion.\n>\n> The author insists that one should distinguish the existence of a thing from apprehending its *quiddity*. Even if a particular existing thing is known, it does not follow that its whole nature is known. Likewise, one may judge “this thing is either existent or nonexistent” while the precise conception of what this thing is remains only imperfectly attained. If the judgment could not occur until a complete quiddity was apprehended, familiar judgments would be impossible.\n>\n> Thus, if one says, “existence is self-evident because the alternatives existent/nonexistent are self-evident,” the argument must not quietly introduce a detailed quiddity of existence into one of the premises. The assent can be necessary without the terms being presented through full philosophical definitions. But neither may one infer from the possession of some use of a term that the *reality* indicated by the term is known in its essence.\n>\n> What is relevant to assent is the understanding of the subject and predicate so far as they enter into the affirmation or denial. It does not require knowledge of all the hidden constituents of either concept. To that extent, the argument can retain its intended force; it does not demonstrate all that a later analysis of the concept of existence might claim. [The last sentence continues into the next author's discussion on I:37.]\n\n**Editorial caution:** The upper distinction between *taʿrīf lafẓī* (verbal explanation) and *taʿrīf ḥaqīqī* (real definition) is source-grounded. The full lower gloss still needs a literal alignment pass; the English above is a working translation of its argumentative clauses rather than certified exhaustive coverage.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":37,"body":"— Is existence shared in meaning or merely in name?\n\n**Upper register — argument 1, opening**\n\n> They have differed over whether the things that exist share **existence** by a single meaning or only by a common word, and whether existence is additional to quiddity or identical with it. To establish that existence is **shared in meaning**, the author offers three considerations. Their presentation is an alert to a meaning that is already evident; the arguments remove what might obscure it.\n>\n> **First:** the soundness of the division of **existence** into the existence of the Necessary and the existence of what is other than the Necessary. What is divided must be shared among the divisions, for division in its proper sense is the joining of what is specific to a class to what is common to the divided subject. Thus division is not the division of a word that happens to have different unrelated meanings, as occurs when a shared sound is used for a [homonymous term]. [The discussion continues onto I:38.]\n\n**Lower register — selected source-based gloss, not fully collated**\n\n> What the author states is most clearly expressed as follows. There is one common conception of existence among all existents, whereas their particularisations are distinct. One may assert the existence of something while remaining uncertain which of its special modes it has. When we consider the Necessary Existent, we may affirm existence while hesitating over some of the characteristics attributed to it. Likewise, in considering a possible existent, we may affirm that it exists without yet determining whether it is a substance or an accident. What is common to the different considerations is therefore not exhausted by their special characteristics.\n>\n> Someone may object that the concept “existence” would then be shared in the same way as “nonexistence.” But this does not follow without a separate account of the respective meanings and their logical divisions. If a single concept may be applied to existents of radically different kinds, the reason must be made precise, not replaced with the assumption that the kinds are identical.\n>\n> An additional dispute concerns whether the commonality lies **in the concept** alone or also in the extra-mental reality of what is predicated. It is one thing to say that “existent” is understood by a single common meaning, and another to say that the existence of the Necessary and the existence of the possible are numerically one thing. The first is what the division is offered to establish; the second does not follow from it.\n\n**Second, short lower gloss beginning near the foot of I:37**\n\n> The phrase **“a meaning”** is attributed to the Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ashʿarī as a point of contrast with those who treat the existence of the Necessary and the existence of a possible existent as distinct in meaning. The transmitted school attribution must be checked independently; this note gives the competing positions rather than verifying a historical consensus. [Its continuation is at I:38.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":38,"body":"— Arguments 1–3 for a common concept of existence\n\n**Upper register — completion of division, then arguments 2 and 3**\n\n> The use of this division does not depend upon a stipulation of linguistic convention and knowledge of that convention. It is therefore not altered merely by variation among languages, unlike an attempted division based only on the ambiguous use of a shared word.\n>\n> **Second:** one can make a decisive judgment of existence while **remaining uncertain about the particular determination** among the types and individuals of existents. If existence were not common in meaning, but had in each case a meaning wholly other than in the next, one could not affirm existence while hesitating about the special determination. Consider a judgment that something exists while one remains uncertain whether it is a substance or an accident. A common content is affirmed although the specific type is not settled. [The original gives further qualifications about whether a special determination belongs intrinsically or accidentally to what is conceived.]\n>\n> **Third:** the permissibility of withholding or denying existence in relation to a quiddity. Were existence simply identical with the quiddity, to deny existence of that quiddity would be to deny a thing of itself. But one can meaningfully consider a quiddity and ask whether it exists, without thereby cancelling the conception of the quiddity. [The upper text carries this last proof forward at I:39.]\n\n**Lower register — selected argumentative clauses, not a full literal collation**\n\n> On **“even though it has one meaning”**: Even when existence is shared as a word, its being shared in meaning must be argued, for two expressions may have different referents under the same utterance. If the shared meaning is established, it does not follow that the particular properties of existent things are the same. Nor does the hesitancy about the particular properties force one to remain hesitant about the general proposition that the thing exists.\n>\n> The denial of existence in relation to a thing can be understood in more than one way: denial of an extramental instance, denial of a particular actuality, or denial of the specific determination with which one had conceived it. These should not be assimilated without explanation. The claim that one conceives a quiddity while being unsure of its actual existence establishes only that the two are distinguishable in thought. It remains a separate question whether existence is something additional in an extramental thing.\n>\n> In this connection the gloss mentions what would follow if “quiddity” and “existence” were taken as interchangeable terms, and why the philosophers' distinctions between *wājib* and *mumkin* cannot be reduced to a merely verbal disagreement. [The rest of this long note continues at I:39.]\n\n---\n\n### Scope and fidelity statement\n\nThe text above replaces earlier site-level topic summaries for **five specific printed leaves** with source-inspected connected English. **Do not count these as five completed all-register leaves:** several longer lower passages are translated only to the extent that their argumentative clauses have been securely read, and some upper joins are marked. The original images and exact physical page numbers are preserved separately. The immediate next source span is I:39 onward, with I:34–38 available for re-collation as a single continuous argument.\n\n---\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":39,"body":"— Is existence additional to quiddity *in thought*?\n\n**Upper register — continuation and three arguments**\n\n> [Completion of the discussion of existence's common meaning.] Otherwise an existing thing might be qualified as existent *in another sense*, and so would not be nonexistent, yet neither would it be existent in the meaning we had intended.\n>\n> **“And [he indicates] its addition to quiddity in the mind”:** That is, the intellect can attend to existence without attending to the quiddity, and attend to the quiddity without attending to existence. There are three grounds for this.\n>\n> **First: “The validity of denying it of [the quiddity].”** That is, of denying existence of a quiddity. For if existence were identical with the quiddity, such denial would be impossible: the denial of a thing of itself is impossible.\n>\n> **Second: [the informativeness of predication].** [The words introducing the second argument occur at the lower end of this upper passage, and their explanation starts on I:40.]\n\n**Selected Arabic:** «على زيادته على الماهية ذهناً»; «لأن العقل أن يلاحظ الوجود دون الماهية والماهية دون الوجود»; «صحة سلبه ... عنها»; «لامتناع سلب الشيء عن نفسه».\n\n**Lower register — source-translated opening lemmas, writer not identified**\n\n> [Continuing the preceding proof by division:] The exhaustive division is also at issue, for [an argument drawn merely from naming] could depend on the conventional generalisation of a figurative usage, which in turn depends upon linguistic convention. But the intended division should be exhaustive *intellectually*, without depending upon those conventions.\n>\n> On **“a thing may be characterised by existence in another meaning”**: This does not depend on there being one common conception of nonexistence. If the meaning of nonexistence were itself multiple, the absence of an exhaustive division would be still clearer: it would be possible for a thing to be described as nonexistent in another sense, yielding an additional division.\n>\n> On **“the intellect can attend to existence without the quiddity”**: The purpose of saying that existence is *additional to quiddity* is to distinguish them **according to conception**, not thereby to establish that they are two separately existing extramental things.\n>\n> On **“one can deny existence of the quiddity”**: An objection is raised in the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*: the negation of the **derived predicate** (*al-mushtaqq*), namely “existent,” from a quiddity does not necessarily negate the **source** (*mabdaʾ al-ishtiqāq*) from which that predicate is derived. It is the latter that the question concerns. For to say that a thing is not existent may deny the instantiation of existence without denying every sense in which existence can be conceived. [The distinction continues in the remaining lower lines.]\n\n**Untranslated or unresolved lower span:** The subsequent argument on whether **nonexistence**, as absence of being, can be predicated of a quiddity and whether this already presupposes the conceptual difference of quiddity and existence is lengthy. Its precise sequence of objection, quotation and counter-objection runs to I:40; it has **not** been translated line by line here. It should be segmented before being treated as a completed marginal note.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":40,"body":"— Why predicating existence adds information; existence and the Necessary\n\n**Upper register — completion of arguments 2–3 and the opposing position**\n\n> **Second: “The informativeness of predicating [existence] of it.”** If existence were just the quiddity itself, the predication would convey no information, for a thing's being predicated of itself does not convey information—this is necessary [or evident].\n>\n> **Third: “One can acquire a conception of [the quiddity].”** Knowledge that existence belongs to it may require acquisition—as when one investigates the existence of jinn or devils—whereas a quiddity's identity with itself does not have to be established in that way.\n>\n> This is the doctrine of the majority of the mutakallimūn and the philosophers, **except that they regard the reality of the Necessary as a particular existence**. **“Thus, according to them, existence is identical with quiddity in the Necessary.”** They argue: if existence were additional to quiddity in the Necessary, then either that existence would require [the quiddity as its cause] or it would require something else. Dependence upon something else is incompatible with the Necessary's independence, because dependence upon another pertains to what is possible. [The argument about the quiddity as cause continues on I:41.]\n\n**Arabic anchors:** «فائدة حمله عليها»; «لأن حمل الشيء على نفسه غير مفيد ضرورة»; «اكتساب تصورها»; «كوجود الجن والشيطان»; «حقيقة الواجب وجود خاص»; «فالوجود عندهم نفس الماهية في الواجب».\n\n**Lower gloss — direct translation of the first objection and its consequence**\n\n> On **“because predicating a thing of itself …”**: The *predication* intended here is also the **derivational predication** (*al-ḥaml al-ishtiqāqī*), as in saying, “The jinn are existent,” not the **predication by identity or correspondence** (*ḥaml al-muwāṭaʾa*), such as when one says, “The jinn are existence.” If the latter were intended, there would be a dispute about whether the predication is properly made at all.\n>\n> As for the philosophers, an opponent points to the possibility of a different kind of additionality: that one may conceive a quiddity, form a second conception of existence, and then consider the quiddity as standing in a relation to what is conceived. One must not confuse a *conceptual* additionality with an independently existing attribute superadded to a reality in the world.\n>\n> If the source's claim is that an extra-mental existence, added to the quiddity, requires an additional cause, the reply depends on what one intends by **“added.”** The proposed regress concerns a real additional existent. It does not necessarily concern an aspect distinct only in the way the mind regards one thing. [The lower writer develops this point over the bottom of I:40 and I:41.]\n\n**Status:** The opening technical contrast, including the two Arabic terms for predication, is translated directly; the remainder of the lower passage is condensed and awaits clause-by-clause review.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":41,"body":"— Does the Necessary have a quiddity other than existence?\n\n**Upper register — continuation of the necessity argument**\n\n> [If the Necessary's existence required something other than itself], it would depend upon another for its existence and would not be necessary through itself. Alternatively, if its own quiddity were what brought that existence about, the quiddity would have to precede its effect in existence. That would entail the quiddity's existing **before its own existence**, which is impossible.\n>\n> It is replied that [the ordering appealed to here must be specified: the priority of an agent to its effect need not simply be the possession of another, separately existing occurrence of the very existence it causes].\n>\n> [There is also the philosophers' point that] the cause of existence in what is possible is distinguished from a material or receptive cause. Existence can be said to subsist in a quiddity *in the intellect* while its extra-mental standing must be treated differently. **“They have a different view”:** They say that the existence of the Necessary is not an additional real feature of an independent quiddity, but its very reality; otherwise a need or composition would arise within that which is supposed to be necessary through itself. [The clause involving the alleged priority of the possible quiddity is compressed; it is retained as a source issue instead of silently emended.]\n\n**Arabic anchors:** «الوجود في الواجب زائداً على الماهية» [from preceding leaf]; «والعلة متقدمة بالوجود على المعلول» [context]; «وأجيب بأن العلة متقدمة بالوجود» [opening of reply]; «وهم يقولون إن ما يلزم تقدمه على المعلول بالوجود هو العلة الفاعلة لوجود الواجب» [word boundaries to recheck].\n\n**Short separate lower marginal note**\n\n> On **“it is the efficient cause, not the receptive cause”**: This is based upon existence's inherence in quiddity, **in the possible existent**, being something conceived in the mind, rather than an extra-mental inherence in the sense of a separable accident. [The printed abbreviation does not identify a historical author.]\n\n**Longer lower gloss — selected source reading and interpretive connective material (NOT full translation)**\n\n> The discussion should distinguish a thing's quiddity, considered intellectually, from that which subsists independently outside the mind. The quiddity of the possible need not be regarded as an independently existent bearer waiting for an additional existence. If it were already existent before receiving existence, the difficulty would indeed follow. But that is precisely what the account being defended rejects.\n>\n> The distinction between **the necessary through itself** and **the possible** is being made with reference to their need for a cause. When one says that the Necessary has no quiddity other than existence, the claim is not that there is literally no intelligible account of it. The intended denial is of a quiddity that would be **ontologically prior** to, or dependent upon something distinct for, its existence.\n>\n> Conversely, if one treats existence and quiddity as *identical in everything*, the familiar distinction between affirming that a thing exists and apprehending what it is becomes obscure. The lower text therefore warns against conflating an external unity with the claim that there can be no conceptual diversity. [The lower discussion continues on I:42; some of these sentences are an argumentative rendering rather than an exhaustive literal proof.]\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":42,"body":"— The Necessary's existence and the possible's quiddity\n\n**Upper register — direct translation of the visible clauses**\n\n> [If a real, additional existence were itself possible and needed another cause,] the supposition of its possibility would require an agent, and the absurd consequence would follow. **But existence, according to those who say that it is additional [to quiddity], is a matter of intellectual consideration** (*amr iʿtibārī*) and does not require [a second] existent thing. There is therefore no difficulty [of this kind].\n>\n> **“Subsisting by itself, distinct from what is other than it, and different in reality from the existence of the possible; therefore …”** That is, because of the difference in reality between these two existences. **“Despite its being singular in”**—that is, the existence of the possible—**“subsisting in quiddity.”** It is not intended that being abstracted from quiddity and subsisting by itself occurs simply *through its own essence*; for then every existence would be abstracted [from quiddity], since what is entailed by a thing's essence cannot differ from it. Thus the existence of the possible would also be abstracted [from quiddity], and that is false, since the quiddity of the possible, considered in itself, admits nonexistence, and possibility is not removed from it.\n>\n> **“But as for the distinction [of the Necessary]”**—its abstraction is therefore a mark distinguishing the existence of the One from [that of the possible]. [The final compressed expression about individuation or distinction needs another reading; the principal argument is preserved above.]\n\n**Visible Arabic anchors:** «لكن الوجود عند القائلين بزيادته أمر اعتباري لا يحتاج إلى موجود فلا إشكال»; «قائم بنفسه مغاير لغيره مخالف لوجود الممكن في حقيقته»; «فلا يراد أن تجرد الوجود عن الماهية وقيامه بذاته ...»; «لأن ماهية الممكن من حيث هي تقبل العدم ولا يرتفع الإمكان عنها».\n\n**Lower register — interpretive argument map, NOT a completed translation**\n\n> If we begin from the fact that the possible needs a cause for existence, and then imagine existence as an additional, independently existent item, a problem arises about the cause of that item. But the intellect can consider the existence of the possible while distinguishing it from its quiddity, without thereby multiplying external entities. This is precisely what one must keep in view when the terms “additional” and “inherent” occur in the argument.\n>\n> The author of the lower discussion maintains that the real philosophical disagreement is not over whether the mind can frame two concepts; that is generally admitted. It concerns **what grounds their distinction** and whether existence is an extramental accident or is the very actuality of the possible thing. One can affirm conceptual distinction without settling the deeper ontological issue by that fact alone.\n>\n> A further objection is directed against the account that the existence of the Necessary is an existence *divested of every quiddity*. If all intelligible description were removed, one would also lose the means of differentiating the Necessary from possible existence. It is important, therefore, that negating an additional quiddity is not construed as negating every possible determination of thought about the Necessary.\n\n**Caution:** This leaf's lower text is especially dense. The paragraphs above follow its major argumentative transitions but **do not constitute a complete literal translation**. The missing lines have not been counted or supplemented from secondary scholastic accounts.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":43,"body":"— The common concept of existence, and the unique reality of the Necessary\n\n**Upper register — the special existence of the Necessary**\n\n> [If the supposed abstraction or independence applied to the possible's existence in the same manner,] **it would not be necessary**. It is not intended here to argue that existence is known necessarily, whereas the reality of the Necessary is, by agreement, unknown. For the inquiry is **not about existence shared in meaning** (*al-wujūd al-mushtarak*), but about **the special existence which differs in reality from the other existences**. The [existence] described as separate [from quiddity] is the second, whereas what is known necessarily is the first.\n>\n> What is meant by **the subsistence of existence in the quiddity of the possible** is its subsistence in it **in the mind, not as a distinct extramental thing**. For no quiddity is established outside the mind in such a way that existence could stand in it [as a separately existing accident]. **“Like whiteness in a body”**—[this comparison is introduced and then qualified:] in the case of a body we are not claiming that the body's quiddity stands there before its existence, so that existence then arrives in it. The discourse thus passes to the meaning of *subsistence* and of the addition of existence, a matter to be considered apart from an image of two independently existing things.\n>\n> **“For the point of our statement that the existence of every thing is additional to it …”** [The sentence is completed on I:44.]\n\n**Visible Arabic anchors:** «لأن البحث ليس في الوجود المشترك بل في الوجود الخاص المخالف لسائر الوجودات في الحقيقة»; «والمجرد هو الثاني والمعلوم ضرورة هو الأول»; «والمراد بقيام الوجود بالماهية في الممكن قيامه بها ذهناً لا عيناً»; «كبياض الجسم»; «لأن القصد بأن وجود كل شيء زائد عليه».\n\n**Separate short marginal notice**\n\n> On **“otherwise that would be impossible”**: [A possible counterreading is rejected] because the [Necessary's] identity is not a quiddity independently established outside the mind. Its standing outside the mind is not a second addition to an already independently existing nature. [One clause about *hudūth* and the philosophers' standpoint is compressed.]\n\n**Long lower note — selected literal clauses and editorial connective reading (NOT a full translation)**\n\n> I say: it seems that, by **the subsistence of whiteness in a body outside the mind**, the disputant intends a sense of *being in another* that concerns the body's being qualified by whiteness. After that, whiteness is not in the external world as a separately existing thing detached from the body. Nonetheless the body and its whiteness are spoken of by two expressions, each referring to what is cognised under a distinct consideration.\n>\n> We should distinguish what is **one externally** from what is **one in concept**. The fact that two concepts can be formed—body and whiteness, or quiddity and existence—does not by itself show that two independent items stand outside the mind. Nor does the fact that one speaks of a thing as existing settle how its existence relates to what it is.\n>\n> The opposing account says that, if quiddity and existence are two real extramental items, then one must consider the existence of each in turn; this threatens a further question about the existence of the existence. The reply invokes a difference between existence as the actuality of a thing and existence treated as if it were another quiddity in need of instantiation. [The writer carries this response onto I:44 and its remaining clauses have not been fully read.]\n\n**Status:** The upper leaf is rendered as an incomplete source-aligned draft because of compressed wording and continuations. The lower note contains a securely identified analogy of **whiteness and body** but the remainder is not yet a certified word-for-word translation.\n\n---\n\n### Work remaining within this batch\n\nThe first-person polemic in the lower registers of I:39–43 runs across printed leaf boundaries; no positive evidence identifies these notes as belonging to Wasīm individually. Several lower paragraphs above are **argument-level renderings or selections**, and should not be counted as completed lower-note translations. The verified original images are supplied with the edition. The next leaf, I:44, is translated below; these earlier lower notes still require their exact Arabic transcription and clause-by-clause collation.\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":44,"body":"— Existence predicated by gradation (*tashkīk*)\n\n**Working translation from the original Arabic scan, printed I:44 = physical PDF page 57.** Upper text includes parenthesised Taftāzānī phrases; the lower gloss is of unverified authorship. This continues directly from I:43.\n\n## Upper register — source-based translation\n\n> Moreover, the **quiddity which is said to receive [existence]** would either be nonexistent—in which case there is a contradiction—or existent—in which case circularity or an infinite regress follows. That is because the addition of existence to quiddity and its subsistence in it are **according to intellectual consideration, not as two independent extramental realities**. It is not like the inherence of whiteness in a body.\n>\n> **“Sharing with it”:** that is, the existence of the Necessary shares with the existence of the possible **“an accident”**—namely the concept of **“being”** (*kawn*), meaning establishment or actuality. This is the common notion of existence **“predicated of [the particular] existences by gradation”** (*bi-l-tashkīk*). For [existence] is prior in the cause to [existence] in the effect; in a substance it is more properly predicated than in an accident; and in something stable or enduring it is stronger than in something nonenduring, such as motion.\n>\n> It is thus a predicate truly affirmed of quiddity and its constituents. But these gradations are not being offered as though the Necessary's very reality were an extra-mental accident inhering in a distinct quiddity. [The last phrase joins the continuation on I:45.]\n\n**Anchors read from the scan:** «وأيضاً الماهية المعروضة إما معدومة فيتناقض أو موجودة فيدور أو يتسلسل»; «وذلك لأن زيادة الوجود على الماهية وقيامه بها إنما هو بحسب العقل دون الخارج»; «وليس كقيام البياض بالجسم»; «الكون»; «المقول على الوجودات الخاصة بالتشكيك»; «لأنه في العلة أقدم منه في المعلول وفي الجوهر أولى منه في العرض وفي القار أشد منه في غير القار كالحركة».\n\n## Lower register — selected direct argument, not yet a full collation\n\n> [Continuing the earlier comparison:] We have said that the relation of **whiteness to a body** differs from the relation of **existence to quiddity**. Here, too, the difference between the two pictures must be considered. The body qualified by whiteness already exists [as a body]; its possessing whiteness is a different determination. But when one speaks of existence's subsisting in quiddity, the quiddity is not assumed to stand outside the mind as already existent prior to the existence being predicated of it.\n>\n> Suppose the quiddity whose existence is in question were already **existent**: that makes the new existence superfluous and requires an explanation of the former existence. If it were **nonexistent**, there would be no independently existing subject in which the alleged extramental existence could inhere. This is why the dispute turns on the meaning of “additional” and “subsisting,” not merely on whether we can frame two concepts.\n>\n> An objection nevertheless remains: in saying that the existence of the Necessary is identical with its reality, are we asserting that it possesses the perfection of being through itself, or that its being is itself without an additional determining factor? The lower writer distinguishes this from the existence of a human being, which becomes actual through factors not identical with its quiddity. [The long lower passage contains a further polemical disagreement about the commentator's reading of *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*. Those lines remain uncollated.]\n\n**Status:** Upper is a connected direct-source first pass, with a compressed final join to I:45. The lower represents only directly recoverable portions of a longer text; it is not a completed all-register translation. No manuscript comparison or independent certification has been performed.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":45,"body":"— Particular existences and the proposed accident of being\n*Physical PDF 58. Upper: connected first-pass translation. Lower: substantial selected translation, not the complete lower leaf.*\n\n### Upper: continuous reading\n\n[Continuing the discussion of] its individuals. Thus his statement that [being] is “predicated of the existences” is made on the view that *kawn* (being) is an accident. But it does not follow from this that the particular existences are numerous, since an accident's multiplicity does not entail the multiplicity of its subject. Each of the particular existences is, rather, said to subsist in itself, since it is a reality different from the other existences.\n\nNevertheless, it is evident that if the particular existence of each thing means its *establishment* (*thubūt*), then holding that *kawn* inheres in it would identify accident and subject: the distinction would be solely that between unrestricted and restricted consideration. Such a distinction does not constitute genuine inherence. If, conversely, the thing's particular existence is something beyond establishment, *kawn* cannot properly be made to mean establishment when predicated of it. This makes clear the weakness of the comparison, “as light [is predicated] of lights”: by this they mean that … [the example is completed on I:46].\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected passages in source order\n\n**On “in the sense of establishment.”** The obvious interpretation would be that this predicate is borne by them only with difficulty; otherwise “existence” would become purely equivocal in expression. One might reply that the predication of *kawn* of the existences is merely derivational, without thereby multiplying [one ultimate] existence. [The final qualification of this brief note is compressed.]\n\n**On “each of the particular existences.”** What is established—that things share the concept of existence—does not require their particular instances to be mutually identical. Animal kinds, for example, share the meaning of animality while differing in specific reality. Yet they do not, merely by virtue of that sharing, constitute one and the same individual animal.\n\n**On “but it does not follow.”** The intention may be to say that absolute existence is predicated of the differing particular existences by a predicate appropriate to their various individual realities. But if existence is asserted of those realities in the sense of their *establishment*, then the same question returns: what distinct reality is supposed to inhere in what other reality? If the absolute is no more than an unrestricted way of considering what is otherwise particular, that conceptual difference does not furnish an accident with a distinct extramental subject.\n\nOne should distinguish a universal considered simply as a common notion from a universal predicated of many individuals. What is shared *in meaning* need not be a numerical thing shared among the individuals. Conversely, what belongs to many individuals in respect of a common concept is not therefore literally present as one thing among them. [The author develops this point with an example of animality; some small-type clauses remain to be checked.]\n\n**On “they did not rightly make *kawn* mean establishment.”** The marginal writer returns to the proposed analogy of light. What is common to different lights is one general meaning, but the lights themselves may differ in their specific realities. Whether the general meaning is itself an additional extramental accident cannot be assumed from that analogy. The next page completes the example.\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «قوله المقول على الوجودات» · «لأن زيادة العارض لا تستلزم زيادة المعروض» · «أن الوجود الخاص لكل شيء إن كان عبارة عن ثبوته» · «كالنور على الأنوار».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":46,"body":"— Light and lights; the attributed identity of existence and quiddity\n*Physical PDF 59. Upper: connected first-pass translation. Lower: extended selected argumentative reading.*\n\n### Upper\n\nThe light of the sun, the light of the moon, and the light of a lamp are different in their realities while participating in the accident of *light in the unrestricted sense*. This [alleged identity] holds “in all”—that is, in the existence of both Necessary and possible beings. [The position is that existence is] “the very quiddity,” as attributed to Shaykh al-Ashʿarī—may God have mercy on him. It appears to be for this reason that he said: “The nonexistent is not a thing.”\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected passages in source order\n\nThe particular existences are said to exist through a reality belonging to them, for existence in the real sense is the effect of the agent in possible things, and in the Necessary it is identical with the essence. Applying the word to the *kawn* said to be an accident is, on this reading, figurative: the mind abstracts an aspect from that which is existence in the proper sense. Existence is what constitutes the principle of effects; a merely abstracted consideration does not become that principle simply because it is called existence. There remains, however, a point requiring examination here.\n\n**On “as light is predicated of lights.”** Perhaps the light common to these lights is to be understood in the manner of a derivative predicate, as *kawn* is said to be predicated of existences; then it would not follow that an additional common luminosity must be a distinct extramental reality. But if one accepts a genuinely common accident, one must explain how its identity and the differences among the particular lights are related.\n\n**On “the accident of unrestricted light.”** Those who hold that the reality of existence is the very principle of effects and those who treat *kawn* as a concept abstracted by the intellect have different objects in mind. The present controversy concerns whether the existence which they say is added to quiddity is itself an entity additional to the reality, or a consideration which the intellect applies to that reality. [The closing portion of the long note continues into I:47.]\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «نور الشمس ونور القمر ونور السراج مختلفة بالحقيقة» · «في الكل» · «من الواجب والممكن» · «نفس الماهية» · «المعدوم ليس بشيء».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":47,"body":"— The nonexistent and the distinction between mental and extramental verification\n*Physical PDF 60. Upper: connected first-pass translation. Lower: selected commentary with unresolved continuation.*\n\n### Upper\n\nFor if existence were the very quiddity, the removal of existence would remove the quiddity, and the nonexistent would be sheer negation. This is unlike the view that existence is additional to quiddity, as maintained by others, such as the Muʿtazila. On that view, the removal of existence does not entail the removal of quiddity, for the removal of an accident does not entail the removal of its subject. That is why they say that the nonexistent is *established*. Those who affirm the additional character of existence and its inherence in quiddity affirm the establishment of quiddity even when it is nonexistent, for they consider the attribution of [an accident] otherwise impossible. The claim that this inherence takes place only in the mind does not require anything more than [mental] establishment within it.\n\nThis does **not** mean that the concept of existence and the concept of a thing are the same; that would be false. Rather, the intended sense is that existence and quiddity do not “each possess their own separate realization in the external world.” That is, the thing is not one distinct extramental reality and the accident called its existence another. Nor is the intended claim that both have one shared extramental realization, since existence, in the disputed sense, has no separate extramental realization at all: there is no thing or identity apart from what is existent. [The next clause, beginning “and the two …,” moves to their intellectual distinction and is continued on I:48.]\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected direct reading\n\nThe marginal writer notes that al-Qāḍī says in his commentary on *Lubb al-uṣūl* that many who deny that the nonexistent is a thing nonetheless hold that existence is additional to quiddity. The attribution of one doctrine cannot therefore be inferred without qualification from the other. This point deserves comparison with the sources.\n\n**On “it has no independent realization in the external world.”** If the author intends to exclude the distinct extramental subsistence of existence as an accident, that is a more defensible claim than saying that there is absolutely no distinction between existence and quiddity. The mind's distinguishing two aspects does not itself establish two extramental entities. But it also does not decide whether existence is something additional in the precise sense at issue. The gloss challenges a slide between the proposition that one thing is *conceived* in more than one way and the claim that there are two *realities* externally conjoined.\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «فإن الوجود إذا كان نفس الماهية فإذا ارتفع ارتفعت» · «المعدوم ثابت» · «لا يتفرد كل من الوجود والماهية بتحقق على حدة في الخارج».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":48,"body":"— Four modes of existence: external, mental, verbal and written\n*Physical PDF 61. Upper: connected first-pass translation. Lower: substantial selected passages.*\n\n### Upper\n\nI say: I did **not** intend by “existence” the relational meaning which has no realization in the external world. I intended that which is the *principle of effects*, namely the actual identity [of a thing]. There is no doubt that, in this sense, it is the very thing itself, as should be evident.\n\nThen existence is divided into the *external* and the *mental*. External existence is existence in the primary sense, that by which the essence of the thing is realized externally—or, rather, it is its very realization. Mental existence is not primary existence in that way: it stands to external existence as a shadow stands to the body. For what is actualized in the mind is a form corresponding to the thing, such that, were that form actualized externally, it would be that thing; just as, were the shadow of a body itself to become a body, it would be that body [in the comparison being proposed].\n\n[Existence in the mind is described as] genuine with respect to the thing's essence and reality, while the division into *verbal* and *written* existence is figurative with respect to that essence and reality. For what exists in an utterance or inscription of “human,” for example, is not the *individual person* [who exists] externally, nor the *quiddity* [represented] in the mind. Rather, what exists in speech is … [completed on I:49].\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected argument\n\n**On “I say …”.** The writer acknowledges that it is quite possible to intend an *iʿtibārī*, considered or abstracted, meaning by the expression “existence.” But if the argument establishes the actuality that serves as a principle of effects, one must not immediately transfer what holds of that actuality to a merely mental consideration. The principle of effects is not made into a distinct inhering accident merely because the intellect distinguishes it conceptually.\n\n**On “if it were actualized externally.”** What is represented in the intellect can be described according to the thing's quiddity without itself constituting another external instance of the same thing. The description of the mental form as a “shadow” is intended to mark its difference of existential manner, not to imply that a person, when thought of, literally becomes a second person subsisting in the intellect. The issue therefore turns on which sense of existence is invoked in the proposition.\n\n**On “in the utterance or inscription.”** If the name “human” is pronounced or written, the external occurrence is the sound or the written marks, not the person or the nature signified. The relation between sign and signified licenses an attribution of existence to the signified “in words” or “in writing,” but that attribution is a different one from the existence of the thing in reality or of its intelligible form in the intellect. [The lower note develops this point through the distinction between signifier and signified across I:49–50.]\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «أقول أنا لم أرد بالوجود المعنى النسبي» · «ما هو مبدأ الآثار وهو الهوية» · «ثم الوجود منقسم إلى ... الخارجي» · «وإلى الذهني» · «وإلى اللفظي والخطي مجازا».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":49,"body":"— The name and its written form; why a mental existent is needed\n*Physical PDF 62. Upper: connected first-pass translation, ending continues to I:50. Lower: selected commentary.*\n\n### Upper\n\n…“the name,” and in writing its inscribed **form**. If the word imposed as a sign for a person is considered in relation to that person, the expression “existence in speech” belongs to this relation of designation, not the person's literal presence in the sound. [A brief compressed qualification is left unresolved.]\n\nAs for the proof of **mental existence**: we make affirmative judgments concerning things that have no realization in the external world, while the judgment must have a subject in some sense. Moreover, [the object of knowledge can be] something in the mind despite the absence of the corresponding thing externally. The argument is developed through affirmations about nonexistent subjects and through universal concepts. [It continues explicitly on I:50.]\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected argument\n\n**On “yes, if the expression were attached to the designated term.”** The gloss warns against treating the attribution of existence to a verbal expression as a discovery of a new reality of the thing outside thought. The word and what it signifies must be distinguished. A name may occur in a sentence or be inscribed on paper even though its referent does not exist externally.\n\nThere is likewise a distinction between the *meaning* of the term and the *image or form* of that meaning in the mind. Philosophers and logicians sometimes designate as “signified” the mental image obtained from the sensible or intelligible thing. Yet the signified is not, simply by being signified, numerically identical to the external individual. The discussion requires attention to which kind of signification is meant.\n\nThe gloss draws the familiar distinctions among the signifying expression, the mental form to which the expression leads, and the thing or reality of which that form is a form. One must not collapse them merely because language permits a predication from one to another. A spoken term's signifying something is not sufficient to confer external actuality upon the thing signified.\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «في اللفظ ... الاسم» · «وفي الخط صورته» · «والدليل على الوجود الذهني».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":50,"body":"— Affirmative judgments about nonexistent things\n*Physical PDF 63. Upper: connected first-pass translation. Lower: selected argument.*\n\n### Upper\n\n[An object of thought need not be] realized externally. If a thing is known, an object of cognition must be present in the mind in the sense required for the judgment; otherwise the alleged cognition would have nothing to be about. This is why affirmative judgments are made **concerning things nonexistent externally**, even when the subject cannot be found there.\n\nFor an affirmative judgment about a thing—for example, “the impossible is more specific than the nonexistent”—requires the thing to be conceived. Likewise, among concepts we find a **universal concept**, which cannot, under its universal aspect, exist as such in the external world; the act of understanding it is an intellectual apprehension. There are also **real propositions** (*qaḍāyā ḥaqīqiyya*) whose subjects do not exist externally at all, such as “Every phoenix is an animal.” Their truth does not require that an actual phoenix be found outside the intellect. [The analysis of these judgments and the inference to mental existence continues on I:51.]\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected passages\n\n**On the argument from affirmative judgments.** A judgment's requiring a conceived subject does not without further argument settle the mode in which that subject exists. It may require a concept in the intellect, but that is precisely what must be differentiated from a real member of the thing's kind. To say that a nonexistent object is represented is not to declare that it exists in the external world.\n\nThe gloss asks whether “mental existence” is meant to be the existence of an entity actually instantiating the represented quiddity, or simply the occurrence of that object's *form* in the intellect. The familiar distinction between the image and its external object is indispensable here. A universal is not externally instantiated *as universal* merely because the mind predicates it of many particulars.\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «أن نحكم ... على الممتنعات في الخارج» · «كقولنا الممتنع أخص من المعدوم» · «ونجده أيضا من المفهومات ... كليا» · «كل عنقاء حيوان».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":51,"body":"— Mental existence and the requirements of judgment\n*Physical PDF 64. Upper: connected first-pass translation, lower: selected.*\n\n### Upper\n\nConsider a proposition such as “Every body is originated.” The judgment requires the subject about which it is made to be conceived, even where that subject is not actually found in the external world.\n\n“Intellection,” understanding, and the distinction made by the intellect concern an intelligible object. If the object were not present to the intellect in some manner, the intended act of intellection would not occur. Hence the act of understanding requires a relation—or *addition* in the sense of a relational consideration—between knower and known, between the intellect and the intelligible. A relation cannot be conceived while both of its termini are conceived as sheer nothingness. What is intended here is therefore the intelligible's **being present in the mind**, not the claim that the thing is established as another independent external object.\n\nYet one should distinguish a relation required for the *act of understanding* from a real constituent added to the substance of the mind. The defender of mental existence argues for the former; the opponent disputes whether the mental object must be said to exist in order for that relation to obtain. [The compressed clauses at the end of the upper register lead into the objection on I:52.]\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected argument\n\nThe writer considers whether conceiving the nonexistent requires that the conceived quiddity itself be a distinct existent in the soul. The conclusion is not immediate. The act of conceiving and what is conceived may be distinguished as intellect and intelligible in one respect, while an additional independent entity is not thereby established. Here again the notion of an *addition* may be conceptual rather than substantial.\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «فالتعقل والفهم والتميز عند العقل» · «أن كان المعقول ...» · «لا يقتضي ... الإضافة» · «في الذهن».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":52,"body":"— Why thinking of heat does not make the thinker hot\n*Physical PDF 65. Upper: connected first-pass translation. Lower: selected.*\n\n### Upper\n\nA further objection arises. If the intelligible existed in the mind, would the mind not have to be qualified by the accidents it conceives—including mutually opposing accidents—and would that not entail the external existence of what is impossible?\n\nWe answer: a thing's existence in the mind is the existence of its **representation** or **shadow**, not its original, extramentally effective existence. **Mental existence does not entail that the mind is qualified by the accidents conceived.** The qualification in question belongs to the original existence to which effects accrue. A person may form a concept of unbelief without becoming an unbeliever merely through that representation; similarly the contemplation of opposed qualities does not require the mind to be simultaneously hot and cold.\n\nNor does conceiving an impossible object confer external being upon it. The mind's having a form which signifies it is not the impossible thing's actually being present in the external world. The verbal comparison with water in a vessel or house is intended to mark the different sense of “in,” not to make mental containment identical with spatial containment. [The author continues into I:53.]\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected argument\n\nA proposed objection is that if the intellect bears forms corresponding to nonexistent, impossible and mutually incompatible things, the intellect seems to be a subject for contradictory dispositions. The answer requires a distinction between the *form through which the object is apprehended* and the form which would physically inhere in a bearer of that quality. Being informed of heat or of colour as an intelligible does not have the causal character of being physically heated or coloured.\n\nThe gloss further examines whether it is legitimate to describe these intelligibles as existing in the mind at all, if all that is meant is a way of considering an object rather than the subsistence of an object additional to the mind. The question is not settled merely by changing the preposition “in” from a local to an intentional use.\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «لو كان المعقول موجودا في الذهن لزم اتصاف الذهن بالأعراض المتصورة حتى المتضادات» · «وهو وجود غير متأصل» · «لا يقتضي الاتصاف» · «كالمؤمن يتصور الكفر فإنه لا يتصف بالكفر بمجرد ذلك».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":53,"body":"— The common meaning of existence and the identity of the mental form\n*Physical PDF 66. Upper: first-pass translation. Lower: selected.*\n\n### Upper\n\nThis is the difference between **mental existence** and existence attributed to words. The mental form is genuinely realized as a form, though those who speak of mental existence describe the form and the thing of which it is a form as united [in cognition]. Those who deny it do not accept that union; for them what occurs in the intellect is only its exemplar or likeness. The difference is not merely that the expression is pronounced or written.\n\nThen, “what is predicated as existence and as thinghood is nothing but **establishment**.” For everything established is a thing and is existent, and conversely. “What is predicated as nonexistence is nothing but **negation**.” Thus everything nonexistent is negated, and conversely. [The concluding qualification crosses to I:54.]\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected argument\n\nThe gloss asks whether both parties use “mental existence” in the same sense. An image may obtain in the mind even for those who deny that the nature represented exists there in the manner of its object. Hence agreement about the presence of a form does not amount to agreement about the ontological status of the nature represented.\n\nThe distinction also turns on whether one takes the mental form to be the very reality of the object *under the aspect of being known*, or an exemplar distinct from it. The controversy cannot be resolved by invoking the mere fact that some image appears to the mind.\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «الاختلاف في الوجود الذهني واللفظي» · «باتحاد الصورة وذي الصورة» · «ليس إلا الثبوت» · «ليس إلا النفي».","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":54,"body":"— The nonexistent, negation, and the disputed “intermediate”\n*Physical PDF 67. Upper: connected first-pass translation where legible; several conditional alternatives in this long passage require a closer collation. Lower: selected.*\n\n### Upper\n\n[The nonexistent is not] a thing or something established. “Thus the nonexistent is neither a thing nor established.” The nonexistent *in the external world* may indeed be something about which one makes a judgment in the mind, since those [under discussion] affirm mental existence. It is not for that reason established as an external thing.\n\nThere is “no intermediary between what is established and what is negated”—that is, between the existent and the nonexistent, or between existence and nonexistence. **This is the position attributed here to the philosophers and the majority of the theologians.**\n\n“Among them are those who affirm it”: that is, who call the nonexistent a *thing* and *established*, because the expression “that which …” can be applied to it and because [something can be] spoken of even when it is not externally realized. They appeal in this connection to usage and to the possibility of making meaningful assertions.\n\nOthers attempt to establish an intermediary as a “state” (*ḥāl*). The discussion distinguishes something wholly negated, which has no realization in itself, from a case in which one can meaningfully ascribe a determination without treating it as an independent existent alongside substance or accident. [The classification branches into conditional alternatives, some of which are compressed in the print; these are **not** silently reconstructed as a complete argument.]\n\n### Lower commentary — unassigned, selected argument, not a full literal translation\n\nThe lower discussion cautions that accepting an intermediate *state* need not mean treating it as a further self-subsisting entity. It disputes what exactly follows from applying words such as *existent*, *nonexistent*, *established* and *negated* to a subject. If one says that a state “is neither existent nor nonexistent,” it must be established whether this is a new ontological division or a technical determination of the terms. The polemic also calls attention to the difference between a thing's not being present in external reality and its being meaningful as the subject of a proposition.\n\nThe small-type text invokes the views of theologians who speak of the *aḥwāl* and those who reject them. Its detailed subdivisions and the final reply remain **untranslated pending literal source collation**; no school or named author is assigned to every position here.\n\n**Selected Arabic anchors:** «المعدوم ليس بشيء ولا ثابت» · «لا واسطة بين الثابت والمنفي» · «وبين الوجود والعدم» · «هذا ما ذهب إليه الحكماء وجمهور المتكلمين» · «ومنهم من أثبتها» · «ويسمى الواسطة».\n\n---","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"},{"page":55,"body":"— Is the alleged intermediary itself existent or nonexistent?\n*Original PDF physical page 68. Upper: directly translated first-pass passage; lower: selected portions only.*\n\n### Upper register — Taftāzānī lemmata and Sanandajī commentary\n\n[The claim concerns an intermediary] **between the existent and the nonexistent**, called a *state* (*ḥāl*). The objection against it is that one may **make existence an attribute of it**. If the existence [of that attribute] were itself to exist, an infinite regress of existences would follow, or an infinity of items would have to come together in a single existence; and that is impossible.\n\n**“Or [it would be] unqualified by the contradictory”**—or by what has its contradictory predicated of it. For, on the supposition that there is an intermediary, nonexistence is more restricted than the contradictory of existence, even if the contradictory is called *non-existence*; and this, too, is impossible.\n\n**“We replied”**: We choose the alternative that [the alleged intermediary] exists; but the regress does not follow, **because its existence is itself**. For every description attaching to *another* is additional to that other, whereas, if it is established for the thing itself, it is not an item added to it—just as nonexistence [is not a distinct additional item in the relevant comparison]. [The sentence continues on I:56.]\n\n**Arabic checkpoints:** «بين الموجود والمعدوم حالا» · «يجعل الوجود صفة له» · «لزم اجتماع أمور غير متناهية في وجود واحد» · «ولو عدم اتصافه بالنقيض» · «نختار أنه موجود ولكن لا يلزم التسلسل» · «لأن وجوده عينه».\n\n### Unassigned lower register — provisional interpretation of selected arguments\n\nOn the alternative, **“or what the contradictory applies to,”** the gloss distinguishes the *contradictory* of a description from what may properly be described by the contradictory. Where nonexistence is introduced as an intermediary, one cannot assume without argument that anything which is not existence must already be an instance of nonexistence in exactly the disputant's restricted sense. Otherwise the interpretation of *being qualified* in the two cases shifts unnoticed.\n\nOn **“and nonexistence,”** the writer insists that the nonexistent may be correctly described in a respect in which *existence* cannot be predicated of it. The reply cannot simply infer the existence of a subject from a statement made about what is nonexistent. [The longer discussion of the two senses of predication is not exhaustively translated.]\n\nA separately ruled lower passage opens on the distinction between **the existent and the nonexistent**. It says that the putative *state* is being treated as a description that is neither existent nor nonexistent *in itself*; its classification cannot be resolved merely by placing it beside common accidents such as blackness and whiteness. The passage continues on I:56, and its author's individual identity is not established.\n\n**Untranslated / uncertain:** The detailed grammatical criticism of *ittiṣāf* in the upper half of the lower register; final words continuing after «الوجود نقيضه»; the remaining ruled lower paragraph.","status":"working earlier draft; margins incomplete"}]);
